## 1. INTRODUCTION
Many natural materials contain empty spaces or voids/pores with sizes ranging from nanometer- to centimeter-scale, such as charcoal and pumice. Inspired by nature, porous solids composed of either inorganic or organic compounds, or a combination of the two, have been made synthetically. Consisting of extended frameworks these porous solids possess numerous accessible internal surfaces, large pore volumes, and molecular-scale open windows. Consequently they are now integral to both the science and the technology of adsorption, catalysis, and molecular separation, and they constitute advanced materials for an extensive range of applications.1−8
We will begin this review with a brief developmental history of porous solids, including the transition from inorganic to organic frameworks. Even before their structures were studied systematically, porous solids were widely used for molecular adsorption, separation, and ion exchange. For example, activated carbon may arguably be the most widely used commercial adsorbent for water purification and gas adsorption. The systematic study of the structures, properties, and industrial applications of porous frameworks began with zeolites, a group of crystalline aluminosilicates with channels and pores. In zeolite structures, aluminate and silicate tetrahedrons are connected by bridging oxygen atoms in a corner-sharing arrangement. The linkage of these tetrahedrons results in rings, cavities, and eventually open frameworks extending three dimensionally. Zeolites are functionally important inorganic materials, which have attracted much attention over the past 70 years, with uses such as adsorbents and catalysts in the petroleum industry.10−12 The triumphs of zeolite technology have stimulated development in the chemistry of other porous frameworks. Various types of porous solids such as auminophosphates (AIPOs), silicoaluminophosphates (SAPOs), ordered mesoporous materials
AJ AJ AK AL AL AL AM AM AN AN AO AO AP AP AP AQ AR AR AR AR AR AR AR AR
(OMMS), and metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have been synthesized and studied over the past two decades.13−16 MOFs are another milestone in the history of porous framework chemistry because of their numerous merits, including diverse chemical properties derived from a wide range of metal ions, controllable pore functions from engineered organic ligands, and varied geometry resulting from reticular chemistry. The main feature distinguishing porous frameworks from denser materials is their porosity. Consequently, the focus of porous framework synthesis is on producing materials with high surface area, high pore volume, and precisely controlled pore size, etc. Although there are several examples of MOFs with ultrahigh porosity, some of which possess experimental Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface areas exceeding \(5000\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\),20 synthesizing porous solids with a surface area higher than that value remains a challenge. One effective and practicable solution is to replace the metals in MOFs with lighter elements such as B, C, N, and O. Development of that concept led to synthesis of nanoporous network polymers being reported in 2002,21,22 followed in 2004 by polymers of intrinsic microporosity (PIMs).23 Both procedures use randomly contorted monomers, which leads to inefficient molecular packing of the polymer chains. Nanoporous network polymers with phthalocyanine and porphyrins as building units possess surface areas in the range \(450{-}950\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(900-\) \(1000\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. The PIMs reported in that work [op. cit.] exhibit BET surface areas in the range 430 to \(850\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), comparable to those for most zeolites and many MOFs.
The synthesis of covalent organic frameworks (COFs) by phenyl boronic acid condensation in 2005 is considered to be the starting point of porous framework construction from entirely organic units that allow topological design.24 Because of their highly open framework and specific composition, COFs can readily achieve surface areas much higher than zeolites and PIMs viz \(>\!1000\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), and several examples with BET surface areas over \(4000\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) have been reported.25,26 In 2007, another two organic porous solids were reported.27,28 The hyper-cross-linked polymers (HCPs) were synthesized from aromatic molecules with bis-chloromethyl groups via Friedel−Crafts alkylation.27 The combination of monomers and synthetic optimization resulted in a series of HCPs with BET surface areas in the range \(600{-}1904\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\). They are derived from porous polymers such as Davankov-type resins.29,30 In contrast, the more-recently reported conjugated microporous poly(aryleneethynylene) networks28 (now known as conjugated microporous polymers, COIPs) follow a different design strategy. For CMP synthesis, monomer molecules with three or more cross-coupling sites are needed (for example, the 1,3,5-substituted benzene nodes ibid). The monomers are connected by rigid strut molecules such as 1,4-substituted benzene nodes via a Sonogashira−Hagihara cross-coupling reaction. In contrast to the porous polymers with their ineffectively packed polymeric chains, the porosity of CMPs originates from the voids among the rigid monomer molecules that extend two- or three-dimensionally (2D or 3D). Although the monomer molecules with three cross-coupling sites ideally lead to the 2D honeycomb structures present in many MOFs and COFs, atomistic simulation indicates that a 3D disordered framework is the most energetically stable structure. The porosity of CMP networks arises from the contortion of the connections between rigid building units, and it can be modified by controlling the size and geometry of the rigid monomers and the struts. A similar situation exists for the
crystalline porous frameworks such as MOFs and COFs. The CMPs thus developed were disordered 3D frameworks with BET surface areas in the range \(522{-}834\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\).
Considering the high surface area of crystalline COF and MOF materials, one might wonder if a high surface area can be only achieved in such highly ordered frameworks. In 2009, a material with a record-high BET surface area for porous aromatic frameworks (PAFs) of \(5600\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), was synthesized by our group. The term "aromatic" originates from the abundant benzene rings in this prototype PAF, called PAF-1. It was synthesized by the Yamamoto type Ullmann coupling reaction of tetrakis(4-bromophenyl)methane (Figure 1). This
irreversible coupling reaction creates strong carbon−carbon covalent bonds, ensuring a stable framework resistant to harsh chemical treatments. During the synthetic design of PAF-1, a default diamond topology is applied to simulate its porosity and demonstrate the origin of its microporous structure. The proposed structure, as simulated, fitted well the empirical adsorption capacity and with a uniformly distributed pore size. From powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) results, we were able to infer that the irreversible coupling reaction leads to the formation of an amorphous framework. Nevertheless, the proposed regular structure is useful theoretically, for functional design of PAFs themselves, for functionalization, and for applications. Compared with porous polymers, which are synthesized by the random connection of monomers, PAFs entail a strategic synthesis, namely porous frameworks, are constructed from rigid building units with predesigned geometries. Therefore, we believe the term "framework" is a closer description than "polymer", from a terminological viewpoint.
Because of its record-high porosity among the known amorphous porous solids, PAF-1 is considered to be another milestone in the engineering of porous organic frameworks (Figure 2). Apart from their high surface area, PAFs also possess several distinct advantages. They can be synthesized from diverse building units via various coupling reactions. On
one hand, the combination of building units with distinct shapes and sizes provides a huge number of porous frameworks differing in surface area, pore volume and pore size etc. On the other hand, specific chemical functionality can be achieved and enhanced not only by the intrinsic chemistry of the building units but also by the connecting struts formed during the coupling reactions. Additionally, PAFs contain aromatic compounds that both ensure the rigidity of the building units and allow sufficient reactivity for postmodification. Last but not least, PAFs are chemically resistant to harshly acidic, alkaline, and humid environments, crucial to their applications in industry.
Because of their uniquely tunable chemistry plus their chemical stability, PAFs are regarded as a new class of porous frameworks, distinct from inorganic porous solids and MOFs. The past decade has witnessed rapid development of PAFs and related materials including, inter alia, covalent triazine frameworks (CTFs), porous organic networks (PONs), porous organic frameworks (POFs), porous polymer networks (PPNs), porous organic polymers (POPs), and covalent organic polymers (COPs). These materials with entirely organic frameworks and strong carbon−carbon linkages were substantially synthesized and their applications have been extensively investigated. Highly porous PAFs have been synthesized to enhance their gas adsorption. Desired functionalities have been introduced to the porous materials either by synthesizing from functional building units or properly modifying the frameworks. They also exhibited specific features which are distinct from MOFs or zeolites, such as their framework flexibility and extreme hydrophobicity of the frameworks. Their extensive applications in adsorption, separation, catalysis, biochemistry, sensing, photochemistry, electrochemistry, and as stimuli-responsive materials have been reported. According to statistics from the Web of Science, up until September 2019, there were over 1600 publications matching one of the following search topics: "porous aromatic frameworks", "porous organic frameworks", "porous organic polymers", "porous polymer networks", "covalent triazine frameworks", "porous organic materials", "porous organic networks", "conjugated microporous polymers", and "microporous polymer networks". However, the terms "covalent organic frameworks" and "metal organic frameworks" were not included in the search. There were more than 55 000 citations for these 1 600-plus publications. Within the last 10 years, the number of annual citations has increased from 19 in 2008 to over 12 000 in 2018 (from Web of Science), reflecting the rapid expansion of this research field. Therefore, we believe it is an appropriate time to provide a comprehensive guide to the essential development in this field over the past decade.
Previous reviews in this field either covered numerous related substances or were intensely focused on a specific topic or application. This is because the PAF field has expanded almost simultaneously with the development of numerous materials with features similar to PAFs, but with new nomenclature and conceptualization, across different research groups. This review will not refer to each of these materials individually, but it is necessary to provide a study of the separate classes of intrinsically similar materials to better understand the current situation. Therefore, this review does not only focus on PAFs but includes other porous organic frameworks that have the following features: (1) framework structures inspired by reticular chemistry and constructed from
rigid building units with specific geometries, (2) building units containing aromatic compounds that yield reactivity and chemistry suitable for specific applications, and (3) the type of connectivity resulting from irreversible reactions and which yields high stability against harsh chemical treatments. For example, the PIMs with their pores between polymeric stacked chains, and the HCPs synthesized from 0D molecules such as benzene, usually do not have regular geometry or the discrete units typical of reticular design. Likewise, graphitic carbon nitrides are prepared from nitrogen-rich compounds through thermal condensation and do not involve participation by aromatic building units. Last, COFs are crystalline frameworks synthesized from reversible reactions and therefore their characterization, modification, and applications are quite different from those of PAFs. Several comprehensive reviews of these other materials already exist.63−68 Furthermore, because of the differences between those other materials in terms of design principles, property modifications, and applications, compared with PAFs, they will not be examined further in the present review.
By examining the research in this field, we found that most papers have focused on the synthesis of new structures and their applications. For applications that rely on the porosity of PAFs, such as gas adsorption and separation, the selection of the building units and reactions for PAF synthesis play the main role in their structure modulation. Alternatively, framework functionalization is a major point of interest for applications that are more related to the chemistry of PAF frameworks such as catalysis, photochemistry and electrochemistry, and stimuli responsivity. That framework functionalization is manifested either direct synthesis from functional building motifs or by post modification. Both porosity and chemistry of the frameworks contribute to the functionality of the resultant PAFs, which in turn leads to their wide applicability (Figure 3). This review aims to concentrate on
four important questions: (1) how to design and synthesize a porous framework with specific porosity (section 2), (2) how can we modify the chemistry of a framework (section 3), (3) what functions can PAFs and related porous frameworks perform (section 4), and (4) what does the future hold for PAFs (section 5)? By addressing these questions, we will shed light on the unique historic development and on the state-of-the-art in synthesis and applications and demonstrate the inimitable properties of PAFs and related porous frameworks.
This should promote further understanding and progress in the field.
## 2. DESIGN PRINCIPLES AND SYNTHESIS OF PAFS
Porous solids can be classified according to their pore size: microporous, mesoporous, or macroporous materials depending on whether their pore diameter is less than \(2~\mathrm{nm}\), between 2 and \(50~\mathrm{nm}\), or larger than \(50~\mathrm{nm}\), respectively (International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry, IUPAC).69 Besides pore diameter, other structural properties of porous solids that relate to their porosity include surface area and pore volume. These properties are strongly linked to their structure and are vitally important in applications. For example, the pore size of zeolites depends on the number of the tetrahedral atoms that form their windows, and two foreign molecules can be separated by the "molecular sieving effect" of a zeolite or MOF when the pore size is in between the size of these two molecules.70 Additionally, selective catalysis based on the "shape-selective effect" can be achieved in those porous frameworks by using specific channel shapes and structures,71 and last, the gravimetric adsorption capacities for hydrogen and other gases are linearly correlated to the surface area of absorbents whereas the binding energies are strongly affected by the pore size and volume.72 Therefore, it is important to understand the principles for design and synthesis of porous solids having a specific porosity.
In the case of PAFs, their structure and porosity are dominated by multiple factors such as the building unit geometries, the mechanism of the reactions, and the degree of framework interpenetration. In this section, the structural design of PAFs and their synthesis will be discussed in detail. Design principles for PAFs will first be generalized by reference to PAF-1 as a case study. The building units and reactions used for PAF synthesis are summarized, along with other investigations. With a view toward creating proposed frameworks, the processes of topology design and "reticular chemistry directed building unit selection" are demonstrated. Following that, the effects of specific PAF synthesis reactions on the surface area of the resultant structures are compared and discussed in terms of the mechanisms involved. Framework interpenetration which induces changes in porosity and adsorption properties is then introduced in terms of empirical findings and computational simulation. Last, we present a brief introduction to some important structural analysis methods that are frequently used to characterize PAFs.
## 2.1. The Formation of Pores in PAF-1
2.1.1. Structural Design. The term "pore" generally represents the lattice or void space in solids or dense phases. In the strictest sense, an absolutely dense phase free of voids does not exist in the real world as there are always spaces between two adjacent atoms. Therefore, the normal meaning of a pore in this field is a space large enough to allow access by single atoms or molecules. For a diamond lattice, the well-known allotrope of carbon with tetrahedrally bonded \(\mathrm{sp}^{3}\) carbon atoms (Figure 4a), the length of the carbon−carbon bond is \(1.54\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\). The distance between any two carbon atoms in diamond is too small to allow any other atoms or molecules to enter and occupy the voids, thus diamond is regarded as a dense compound. If the carbon−carbon bonds were replaced by larger struts such as phenyl, biphenyl, and \(p\)-terphenyl, the distances are significantly extended and three porous frameworks with less effective stacking of the molecules can be
proposed (denoted as P1, P2, and P3, respectively). In the diamond-type structures of P1, P2, and P3, the distance between any two carbon atoms, spanning the motifs, are 6.16, 10.25, and \(14.49\mathrm{~\AA},\) respectively. The expanded connections lead to the formation of pores of diameter 7, 14.8, and \(23\,\mathring\mathrm{A}\) for P1, P2, and P3, respectively (Figure \(^{4\mathrm{b-d})}\) .31
Computational studies with a combination of first-principle calculations and grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulation also confirmed that P1 to P3 have porous framework structures, according to the simulated \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms. As simulated, P1 and P2 have micro porous structures with BET surface areas of \(1880\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and \(5640\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively, and P3, with \(p\) -terphenyl as the struts, is mesoporous. Theoretical considerations indicated that the extension of the links in a network would give rise to the formation of pores and that the pore size can be controlled by the length of the linkages.
2.1.2. Synthesis of PAF-1. Motivated by auspicious simulation results and topology analysis, progress has been made in synthesizing designed PAF structures. Effort initially focused on the P2 type of structure because it could be achieved by construction from a single set of building units. To form the strong, long-range carbon−carbon bonds, effective cross coupling reactions have been screened and reaction conditions optimized. The nickel(0)-catalyzed Yamamoto-type Ullmann coupling reaction was finally selected for the poly condensation of tetrahedral shaped building units, tetrakis(4- bromophenyl)methane. The bromine positions at the tetrahedron's vertices are dehalogenated, and carbon−carbon bonds are formed to achieve the head-to-head condensation of tetrahedral building units. The resultant product was named PAF-1, and the successful poly condensation of the tetrahedral building units at the desired sites was confirmed by comprehensive characterizations such as FTIR and solid-state NMR. The PXRD pattern suggested that PAF-1 is amorphous. This amorphous framework arises because PAFs are synthesized via irreversible coupling reactions, a kinetic process not allowing reconnections, which is different from a thermodynamically controlled process. However, because of the definitive reaction mode of the Ullmann coupling reaction and the high symmetry of the building units, PAF-1 possesses regular microporosity. The experimental \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption suggested that PAF-1 possessed the highest reported Langmuir
surface area of \(7100\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at that time. The hydrothermal stability of PAF-1 was tested in boiling water for a week, and the result indicated a superior stability of PAF-1 compared to some MOFs with high surface areas. PAF-1 is therefore suitable for the application of adsorption and storage of important industrial gases including hydrogen and carbon dioxide as well as organic vapors such as benzene and toluene.
## 2.2. Topology-Directed Design of PAF
The diverse structures of the conventional porous frameworks including zeolites and MOFs are usually obtained heuristically. In zeolite frameworks, the angles of the oxygen bridges that connect the adjacent tetrahedral atoms can be varied to create different structures.73−75 Although the targeted structural design and porosity control of MOFs can be achieved using the strategy of isoreticular synthesis with expanded or decorated ligands (IRMOFs),76,77 the structural diversity of the secondary building unit (SBU) simultaneously enriches the MOF structures and impedes structural prediction. The building units of PAFs that are used in their design and synthesis have individualized geometry and the chemical connectivity in the coupling reactions is explicit. Therefore, topology-directed design of PAF structures can be readily achieved.
Topology concerns the geometry of the building units and the manner of their connection. The building units are effectively just nodes, describable by the numbers of their connecting sites and their relative location in space, regardless of the specific motifs in the structure. For example, the structure of P2 (Figure 4c) can be visualized as adjacent tetrahedrons connecting at the vertices in a head-to-head fashion, namely between the core building unitstetraphenylmethane molecules with their tetrahedral geometry. Some useful databases such as the Reticular Chemistry Structure Resource (RCSR) have been established and they classify the topology of a framework and describe it in terms of a combination of specific vertices and links.78 The four structures in Figure 4 can be described as a dia net, where the bold, three-letter lower-case symbol represents the network's topology as recorded in RCSR. For any given network structure, the database can provide information about which combination of vertices it uses from the building units in the design of its top-down topology-directed framework.
In contrast to the structure of diamond, which has its tetrahedral nodes connected by carbon−carbon bonds and is regarded as a nonporous material due to the dense packing of carbon atoms, PAFs with diamondoid networks exhibit satisfactory porosity for their potential applications. From the examples shown above (Figure 4), it can be concluded that if the connecting carbon−carbon bonds are replaced by longer struts such as phenyl rings or other bulky motifs, it causes less effective stacking of the building units and creates porosity in the organic network. For any given topology, a porous network structure can be theoretically reduced into its composite building units. The geometry and size of these building units strongly influences the porosity of the constructed porous organic framework. It is necessary to consider several characteristics of the building units used for PAF syntheses. They can be classified according to their molecular shapes and steric configurations around the connecting sites.
On the basis of the similar strategy, PAF-5 was successfully synthesized from the triangular building unit, 1,3,5-tris(4- bromophenyl)benzene with \(D_{3}h\) symmetry.79 The proposed
structure of PAF-5 is thus considered as hcb topology. Similarly, PAF-6 in hcb topology was successfully synthesized from melamine as the three-connected planar triangular building unit and piperazine as the two-connected linear building unit. These examples further demonstrated that the symmetry of the molecular building units directly affects the pore openness and dimension, which are determinative for the properties of PAFs. Subsequent researches on the synthesis of PAFs with various topologies such as from linear, triangular, tetrahedral, planar tetragonal, and cubic organic molecules have been reported. On the basis of the empirical evidence, general principles about topology-directed design of PAFs were comprehensively summarized and the details were referred to a feature article elsewhere.
## 2.3. Building Units for PAF Synthesis
2.3.1. Geometries of Building Units. To date, a profound array of porous frameworks unique in their surface area and pore size distribution have been synthesized from building units of different geometry and size. The building units utilized in these are classified by their geometries, for example, cubic, prismatic, tetrahedral, square, triangular, and linear. The PAFs synthesized from these building units are summarized below.
2.3.1.1. PAFs with Cubic Core Building Units. The most frequently used cubic building unit is polyhedral oligomeric silsesquioxane (POSS), which is a perfect nanobuilding unit for constructing three-dimensional materials with potential applications in photonics and electronics. The silsesquioxane core is frequently observed in zeolite structures as a building unit called double four-member ring (D4R), therefore it is also possible to build PAFs with zeolite structures with D4R motifs. For example, a porous framework named JUC-Z1 is synthesized from \( p \)-iodo-octaphenylsilsesquioxane (I8POS) as the sole building unit via an Ullmann coupling reaction. Direct linking of D4R at its vertices is possible to generate the LTA or ACO topologies. The pore size distribution suggests that JUC-Z1 possesses two narrowly distributed pores, 11.6 and \( 20.0\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}} \) wide, which is in good agreement with its presumed zeolite LTA topology. In the situation where the I8POS building units were connected by a phenyl ring via the Suzuki reaction, the framework obtained (PAF-12) is predicted to also have the LTA topology, according to the results of the pore size distribution. Meanwhile, the pore size distribution and the \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) adsorption isotherm both support the production of mesopores, indicating that the pore size of porous frameworks can be modified by controlling the size of the building units.
The POSS units can be bridged by multiple struts with different sizes and shapes, depending on the starting molecules and the coupling reactions applied. The POSS building units with terminal bromophenylethenyl groups at the apical sites of the cubes are connected by linear struts to produce a framework with proposed ACO topology from a zeolite framework database. Alternatively they can be connected by a triangular strut, leading to the framework with the-a network from RSCR. Approaching from a different direction, the \( \mathrm{Ni}(0) \)-mediated Yamamoto-type Ullmann coupling reaction was applied to the same building unit of bromophenylethenyl-terminated POSS. The X-ray powder diffraction pattern showed a crystalline structure but with peak broadening, and the framework was assigned the ACO topology. Because of the presence of the D4R motif and the specific physical and chemical properties of silsesquioxanes, POSS is widely used for
cubic building units to create hybrid porous frameworks, either alone or together with other bridging molecules with tunable porosity and properties.
2.3.1.2. PAFs with Prismatic Core Building Units. The syntheses of PAF materials using prismatic building units are reported less often than with other building units, most likely because of the complexity of synthesizing organic molecules with prismatic geometry. However, the few studies to date have shown that comparing the prismatic building units with other types provides insight into synthetic procedures for PAFs. A so-called "building unit engineering strategy" was applied in synthesizing PAF-101 from a prismatic building unit, which is the dimer molecule of the tetrahedral building units used for PAF-1 (Figure 5). The product exhibited comparable
porosity and better structural regularity than PAF-1 according to the shape of the adsorption isotherms and the pore size distribution alone. Compared to the PAFs synthesized from tetrahedral building units, much more narrowly distributed pore size and fewer structural defects are observed when using the dimer-type prismatic building units. This can be attributed to the significantly reduced coupling steps during polymerization. The building unit engineering strategy is also capable of synthesizing PAFs with unequal strut lengths, thus modifying porosity and introducing structural diversity into PAFs.
KPOP-1 and KPOP-2 (KPOP = KAUST's POP) were synthesized from prismatic building units similar to those for PAF-100 and PAF-101, but the central \( \mathrm{sp}^{3} \) C atoms were replaced by Si atoms. KPOP-3 was synthesized from a trigonal prism building unit containing triphenylbenzene. The KPOPs synthesized from twisted prism building units, and PAF-100 and PAF-101, all possess surface areas much larger than 5000 \( \mathrm{m}^{2} \) \( \mathrm{g}^{-1} \) and exhibit high methane uptake capacity at high pressure and room temperature.
2.3.1.3. PAFs with Tetrahedral Core Building Units. The molecules with a tetrahedral shape include tetraphenylmethane and its derivative containing alternative central atoms (such as Si, Ge, B and P), tetrakisphenyladamantine and spirobifluorene (Figure 6d−f). Building units with tetrahedral cores have high symmetry which makes them popular in PAF, being preferred for creating 3D frameworks with predictable topologies.
Encouraged by the successful design and synthesis of PAF-1 with its exceptional porosity properties, enthusiasm has increased for research into the porous frameworks with tetraphenylmethane core building units and diamond-like structures by both theoretical simulation and experimental syntheses. Multiple methods of computational simulation have
been used to predict new PAF structures and to investigate their potential properties.94−101 In addition to the structures of P1, P2, and P3, a diamond-like structure with its carbon−carbon bonds replaced by quadriphenyl was later proposed, and the hydrogen storage performance of all four were simulated.94 A further set of PAF structures, PAF-30X were proposed, where X represents the number of phenyl rings that link two \(\mathrm{sp}^{3}\) carbons together. The simulation analysis based on multiscale calculations combined with first-principle calculations and GCMC indicates that the diamondoid frameworks with three phenyl rings (P303) and four phenyl rings (P304) both possess mesopores with diameters of 2.08 and \(2.86~\mathrm{nm}\), respectively. The simulation also suggested that PAF-303 and PAF-304 would have significantly high gravimetric hydrogen uptakes and they are expected to yield promising applications for hydrogen storage.
Computational simulations have proposed many framework structures with tetraphenylmethane core building units. At the same time, experimental investigations also achieved great success for syntheses of PAF materials with permanent porosity. Synthesis of the porous frameworks PAF-303 and PAF-304 was complicated. PAF-303, with an odd number of connecting phenyl rings, can only be synthesized via copolymerization of tetrahedral building units and phenyl struts. Tetrakis(4-bromophenyl)methane and phenyl diboronic acid were copolymerized via the Pd-catalyzed Suzuki cross coupling reaction. The resultant EOF-6 (EOF = element organic framework) possesses permanent porosity with a surface area of \(1380~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). A porous framework with the PAF-304 simulated structure was synthesized by replacing the phenyl diboronic acid by biphenyldiboronic acid, and the product was named PAF-11.103
Apart from the dia-topology structures formed by connecting only tetrahedral building units, or including linear bridging struts, other connecting motifs can participate in the polymerization and lead to different predicted topologies. PAF-2 was synthesized from the building unit tetrakis(4-cyanophenyl)methane.104 The nitrile groups in the building units were converted to the planar triangular \(\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{3}\) rings via cyano cyclotrimerization. The assembly of tetrahedral and triangular building units would generate ctn or bor nets. The structures of both such networks have been simulated, and their pore size distribution was compared with experiment. PAF-2 exhibited a single pore size uniformly distributed at averaging around \(1.06~\mathrm{nm}\). Only the ctn net showed a single pore size, and the simulated bor net has two kinds of cavities with different pore sizes. Therefore, the structure of PAF-2 was thought to be a ctn net. Similar combinations of tetrahedral and triangular building units have been reported, such as PAF-18105 and PAF-30.106
Tetrahedral building units can consist of molecules with alternative central atoms, other than the \(\mathrm{sp}^{3}\) carbon of tetraphenylmethane, for example, Si and Ge. PAFs synthesized from such building units were reported by two groups independently in 2011, and these were achieved via a Yamamoto-type Ullmann reaction but under slightly different synthetic conditions.45,107 PAF-3 with a Si central atom and PAF-4 with Ge as the central atom were synthesized in dehydrated DMF at \(80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). PAF-3 and PAF-4 possess BET surface areas of 2932 and \(2246~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively.45 The same building units coupled in a DMF/irreversible cross coupling THF solvent mixture at room temperature lead to PPN-3 and PPN-4 (PPN = porous polymer network). Surprisingly, PPN-3 and PPN-4 exhibited much higher surface areas (i.e., the BET surface area of PPN-4 reached up to 6461 \(\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) than for PAF-3 and PAF-4. This indicated that the synthetic conditions greatly influenced the porosity of the frameworks.107
With regards to tetraphenylmethane, the central carbon from group 14 can be replaced by other elements from group 13 or group 15. Such exchanges would lead to the formation of ionic building units with tetrahedral geometry. Two charged phosphonium microporous polymer networks (CPN-1 and CPN-2) with cationic frameworks were synthesized in the presence of the building unit tetrakis(4-bromophenyl)-phosphonium bromide.108 Similarly, the tetrahedral building units with central boron atoms were exploited for the synthesis of anionic frameworks.47,109 Molecules such as lithium tetrakis(4-bromo-2,3,5,6-tetrafluorophenyl)borate and lithium tetrakis(4-iodophenyl)borate (LTIPB3), were copolymerized with other building units with linear, triangular, and tetrahedral geometries via Sonogashira coupling. The usage of ionic building units enriches both the prototype and application repertoires of PAFs, for example, to include greenhouse gas storage and radioactive element capture.
The geometry of the building unit with a spirobifluorene core depends on the position of the reacting sites in the molecules. The most common spirobifluorene derivatives are \(2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}\)-substituted molecules, which lead to planar building units. To achieve tetrahedral building units with a spirobifluorene core, the substituted reacting groups should be located at the positions \(3,3^{\prime},6,6^{\prime}\). Location of the reacting groups at specific positions can be achieved by occupying the more reactive \(2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}\) positions with inert substituents. This
approach can produce several different porous materials with tetrahedral building units.\(^{110}\)
2.3.1.4. PAFs with Planar (Square and Triangular) Core Building Units. The above discussion has shown some building units with three-dimensional geometries. These cores are only derived from a limited range of molecules due to restrictions in synthesis, although the design of new molecules continues. In contrast, molecules with planar geometries, such as square and triangular molecules, are more commonly available through organic synthesis. There are also more-planar geometries of building units used for PAF syntheses. Porphyrin is one of the most popular cores for square buildings units, and many porous frameworks have been synthesized from the self-polymerization of porphyrin derivatives\(^{111-113}\) or by copolymerization of porphyrin derivatives and other building units.\(^{114-116}\) Other core molecules for square building units include pyrene,\(^{117-119}\) tetraphenylethylene,\(^{120-122}\) and \(^{2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}}\)-substituted spinobifluorene.\(^{123-125}\) Many PAFs have been also synthesized from building units with triangular core molecules, for example, inter alia, 1,3,5-substituted benzene,\(^{126,127}\) 1,3,5-triophenylbenzene,\(^{79,128}\) triphenylamine,\(^{129-131}\) s-triazine,\(^{106,132}\) and triptycene.\(^{133,134}\)
The varied geometries of building units provide a diversity of structures and porosities of PAFs and other porous organic frameworks. Self- and copolymerization of different building units and copolymerization of building units with linear struts, results in a range of topologies. Common building units with different geometries and their combinations, yielding different frameworks, are summarized in Figure 6 and Table 1.
2.3.2. Size Effect of Building Units. Enlargement of the pore size and pore volume of MOFs is achievable through the isoreticular strategy of using expanded ligands.\(^{76,135}\) This is reinforced by computational simulation that shows PAFs with larger building units or longer connecting struts having larger pores.\(^{94,95}\) A series of PAFs (PAF-111, -112, and -113) have been synthesized through the cross-coupling reaction of a tetrahedral building unit with different triangular building units. The change in size of the triangular building units from 1,3,5-substituted benzene to 1,3,5-triphenyl substituted benzene with larger molecules leads to more pores at the mesoporous range.\(^{128}\) This example demonstrates the general strategy of synthesizing PAFs with enlarged porosity through the size effect of the building units.
With the intention of expanding the pores in the dia framework, bromide functionalized tetrahedral building units were cross coupled with 4,4′-biphenyldiboronic acid to give PAF-11.\(^{103}\) Theoretically the product is an expanded diamondoid structure with the carbon−carbon bonds of diamond replaced by four benzene rings to give a mesoporous framework. However, the pore size distribution of PAF-11 is in the range \(0.5{-}5\ \mathrm{~nm}\), indicating high disorder in the structure. In contrast, the structure predicted for PAF-70\(^{136,137}\) is identical to PAF-11, but the former is synthesized from boronic acid functionalized tetrahedral building units and \(4,4^{\prime}\)-dibromobiphenyl via the Suzuki−Miyaura cross coupling reaction. The alteration of the reactive sites in the building units unexpectedly resulted in a change in porosity. PAF-70 showed a typical type-IV nitrogen adsorption isotherm with a pronounced hysteresis loop, which is characteristic of a mesoporous structure. A narrowly distributed mesopore size, centered around \(3.8\,\mathrm{~nm}\), was calculated from the isotherm, which is in accordance with the simulated pore size of \(3.6\ \mathrm{nm}\). This exemplifies that predicted mesoporous structures can be
obtained by selecting suitable building units and synthetic pathways. PAFs with mesopores are ideal for channel modification to accommodate bulky active sites for potential catalytic applications.
2.3.3. Framework Interpenetration. Although PAF-1 has a high surface area, in agreement with simulations, there are many diamondoid-structured PAFs that have lower surface areas than PAF-1. This indicates that there may not only be inefficient packing of building units, which leads to the high porosity of PAF-1 but other factors too. Framework interpenetration is the repetition of the overlapping networks within the unit cell, and it is commonly observed in MOFs and COFs.\(^{139}\) Usually the presence of many empty voids tends to accommodate additional frameworks, coalescing together by interpenetration. The interpenetration dramatically reduces the framework porosity as the density of the total framework increases proportionally with the number of coalesced frameworks (interpenetrating degrees). A good match between the experimental surface area and the simulated one for PAF-1 indicates the absence of an interpenetrating framework, possibly because the voids formed in PAF-1 are too small to allow formation of extra frameworks. A coarse-grained molecular dynamics simulation revealed that although the aromatic building units for PAF syntheses are likely to form interpenetrating frameworks due to the strong interactions between them, the Yamamoto-type Ullman coupling reaction forms an intermediate with steric hindrance that prevents interpenetration in PAFs.\(^{139}\) Also, the solvent plays a spacing filling role that prevents interpenetration during PAF-1 synthesis.\(^{140}\)
The building units based on the tetrakisphenyladamantane are larger than those based on tetraphenylmethane. Therefore, it could be expected that the bulky adamantane core would tend to weaken the packing between buildings units due to steric hindrance and thus restrain possible interpenetration in the diamondoid porous frameworks. Investigations into the syntheses of porous polymer networks (PPN-1, PPN-2, and PPN-3, Figure 7) allowed observation of the effect on framework interpenetration control using the bulky tetrakisphenyladamantane building units. Compared to PPN-1, which is synthesized from tetrakis(4-ethynylphenyl)methane, PPN-2 and PPN-3 with tetrakisphenyladamantane building units
exhibited a higher surface area than that of PPN-1. However, if these three frameworks have the theoretical noninterpenetrating structures as modeled, then they would have comparable simulated surface areas. PPN-3 synthesized via Yamamoto reaction possesses a BET surface area up to \(2840\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), but the surface area for PPN-1 is only \(1249\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Furthermore, both PPN-2 and PPN-3 exhibited more pores above \(1\ \mathrm{nm}\) diameter than did PPN-1. Therefore, PPN-1 is most likely an interpenetrating framework.97
It is possible to tune the porosity of a porous framework by controlling the interpenetration. As shown in Figure 8, smaller
ligands attached to the building units lead to the formation of interpenetrating frameworks. Replacing the ligand by bulky molecules hinders the tendency to form interpenetrating frameworks. Removal of the bulky ligands from the porous framework enhanced their porosity, including increased pore size, high surface area, and high microporous volume.141
2.3.4. Computational Simulation in PAF Design and Synthesis. Computational simulation promotes the understanding of PAF design and synthesis in many aspects. Multiple methods such as first-principle calculations, GCMC, density-functional-based tight-binding (DFTB) method, quantized liquid density functional theory (QLDFT), and coarse-grained
molecular dynamics simulations have been applied in the investigation of PAFs on their design, synthesis, and property. On one hand, new PAF structures with any given porous network topology and different motifs are proposed and their corresponding properties are simulated. For instance, in order to achieve PAFs with diamondoid topology, different building units with phenyl derivatives as linkages, such as pyrene, perylenetetracarboxylic acid (PTCDA), and diphenylacetylene (DPA), were simulated. With regard to PAF-304 (here denoted as PAF-qtph), which contains the longest phenyl linkers and largest pore volume, the effect of interpenetration on the physical properties including density and hydrogen storage capacity were also investigated. The QLDF1 method, which takes into account interparticle interactions and quantum effects, also suggested that PAF-304(PAF-qtph) would exhibit high \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake at room temperature and 100 bar.95 Using density functional theory and GCMC simulations, a diamond-like carbon allotrope with acetylene motifs were proposed. The acetylene motifs in the proposed structure provide large number of adsorbing sites for hydrogen and a 14.12 wt % hydrogen uptake was achieved at \(77\ \mathrm{K}\) and 2.0 MPa.96 On the other hand, known building units would lead to different topologies and new frameworks by simulation. The tetrahedral building unit of tetrakis(4-bromophenyl)methane was used to replace the tetrahedral silicon sites in zeolites and 115 organic PAF structures were consequently proposed by GCMC simulation. Their adsorption performance for hydrogen, methane, and carbon dioxide were screened, and some promising materials were recommended.97
Although PAF simulations based on ordered frameworks were extensively proposed, most of the PAFs are amorphous due to the irreversible cross coupling reactions of synthesis process. In this case, the molecular dynamics simulation can reveal the most energetically stable disorder structure. Trewin et al. have compared a number of possible 4-connected frameworks from the building unit of PAF-1 and suggested the amorphous structure was in accordance with the experimental results such as PXRD pattern and porosity properties.140,142 Molecular dynamics is also employed to monitor the growth and assembly procedure of several PAFs. Building units with varied sizes, concentrations, and interactions were taken into account during the simulation to explain the origin of the high porosity of PAFs.139 The research inspires the design of new
building units for high porous organic frameworks. Also, on the basis of the simulated amorphous structure, property investigation such as the guest molecule diffusing procedure has been carried out and the interaction between the framework and guest was investigated.143 Both ordered and amorphous networks from computational simulation provide the insight view into the framework chemistry at molecular scale.
## 2.4. Reactions
2.4.1. Yamamoto Type Ullmann Coupling Reaction. To construct porous frameworks it is necessary to find a suitable reaction that connects the building units at their reacting sites. The reactions for PAF syntheses should be able to produce rigid, stable connections such as carbon−carbon bonds and aromatic cyclic compounds. Meanwhile, chemical reactions with unambiguous products facilitate topology-directed synthesis. Furthermore, the preference is for high reaction efficiency: a material yield with greatly improved polymerization and porosity. A wide variety of reactions have been exploited for PAF syntheses using rigid organic building units with multiple reaction sites.
For example, the \(\mathrm{Ni}(0)\) catalyzed Yamamoto type Ullmann reaction is one of the most commonly used coupling reactions for PAF synthesis. That reaction involves the dehalogenation poly condensation of aryl halides catalyzed by the nickel(0) complex \([\mathrm{Ni}(0)\mathrm{L}_{m}]\) . The aryl halides such as \(\mathrm{Ar-Br}\) or \(\mathrm{Ar-I}\) first underwent oxidative addition with \(\mathrm{Ni}(0)\mathrm{L}_{m},\) and complexes I and \(\mathbf{II}\) were formed (Figure 9a). The disproportionation of I and \(\mathbf{II}\) results in equivalent amounts of nickle(II) halogenide and complex III. Finally, the reductive elimination of III regenerates the \(\mathrm{Ni}(0)\mathrm{L}_{m},\) and the aryl−aryl compound is obtained as the addition product (Figure 9a).144 It is noteworthy that excess \(\mathrm{Ni}(0)\mathrm{L}_{m}\) is required, as one nickel(0) complex is consumed in the disproportionation step with the elimination of every two halogen atoms \(\mathrm{Ni^{0}}\) to \(\mathrm{Ni^{II}}\) transformation shown in red in Figure 9a). The reaction mechanism and the fact that no residual halogen was detected in the PAFs synthesized from Yamamoto-type Ullmann reactions both indicate that this type of reaction is efficient in eliminating halogen atoms from the building unit molecules.31 This is one of the reasons why PAF-1 possesses an unexpectedly high surface area, otherwise the heavy halogen terminal atoms would dramatically decrease the surface area.142 Meanwhile, the Yamamoto-type Ullmann reactions usually exhibit high yields and the nickel-based catalysts are readily removed by acid treatment. These features of Ullmann coupling all contribute to the produced PAFs having high porosity, making the reaction very popular. In contrast, other typical coupling reactions may not actively remove the reacting sites completely. For example, in the Pd-catalyzed Sonogashira reaction, the Pd-based catalyst participates in the reaction at low dosages. Only some of the halogen atoms form the additional intermediates with the catalyst and react with the acetylene, which in turn means that reactions only occur between some of the building units. The differences between the mechanisms of Yamamoto-type Ullmann coupling and Pdcatalyzed Sonogashira coupling are shown and compared in Figure 9b. Furthermore, the Pd residues are trapped within the solid frameworks and are difficult to remove after the reaction. To date, almost all of the porous organic frameworks with BET surface areas higher than \(4000\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) have been synthesized via either the Yamamoto-type Ullmann reaction or under
optimized conditions.31,45,46,92,107 This further supports the standpoint that the Yamamoto type Ullmann reaction is efficient for the syntheses of porous frameworks with ultrahigh porosity.
2.4.2. Other Coupling Reactions. Besides the Yamamoto type Ullmann reaction for PAF-1 synthesis, other reactions have been exploited for the syntheses of PAFs. Suzuki− Miyaura cross coupling is used for the aryl−aryl formation from aryl boronic acid and aryl halides with Pd-based catalysts.145 Sonogashira cross coupling is another commonly used reaction for PAF syntheses. It entails the copolymerization of terminal alkynes with aryl halides and a palladium catalyst, activated by a copper species.146 It is one of the most frequently used reactions for forming carbon−carbon bonds and for many conjugated microporous polymers (CMPs).147 Compared to the homocoupling Ullmann reactions, the cross coupling reactions have the advantage of connecting two kinds of building units with distinct geometries. Therefore, the latter are suitable for the syntheses of PAFs with diverse topologies. Moreover, developing a range of reactions provides more avenues for modifying the porosity of PAFs. Coupling reactions such as the Heck reaction,148−150 1,3-diyne formation,151 Stille coupling,152,153 and others are also used to form carbon−carbon connections.154−158 These coupling reactions are widely used for PAF syntheses because of their high productivity, and the methodologies for preparing the building units with desired reacting sites are well established. Compared with the Yamamoto-type Ullmann reaction, which is sensitive to the synthesis conditions and to the substituting groups in the substrate, reactions that are tolerant to the building units with various functional groups, are those desired for direct synthesis of PAFs with functionality. For example, ethynyl functionalized tetrahedral building units of tetrakis(4-ethynylphenyl)methane and hydroxyl functionalized tribromobenzene were copolymerized via the Sonogashira reaction to give hydroxyl functionalized PAF-18-OH.105 Similarly, replacing the hydroxyl functional building unit by dibromobenzoic acid resulted in the carboxyl-infixed PAF-26-COOH.159 Both materials have electron donating sites that bond to light metals, such as Li, Na, and Mg, to improve their hydrogen storage capacities.
2.4.3. Cyano Cyclotrimerization. Apart from the linear connection that is formed by carbon−carbon bonds or carbon−heteroatom connections, another connecting mode is achievable by the formation of aromatic cyclic compounds. The covalent triazine framework (CTF) was first reported by Arne Thomas et al. in 2008.33 They reported the cyano cyclotrimerization of several aromatic dinitrile compounds such as terephthalonitrile through isothermal syntheses that occurs in molten \(\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}\) . Although the starting materials are only the linear molecules with two reacting sites, a triazine central motif was produced during the reaction, leading to a two-dimensional hexagonal structure with AAA stacking layers. The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherm suggested that the resultant CTF-1 had permanent porosity with a surface area of \(791\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) . Other starting materials with nitrile groups have also been used to produce porous frameworks. In this case, the permanent porosity in porous organic frameworks can be attributed to the formation of aromatic cyclic motifs with synthesis extension along the backbone of the starting molecules. The aromatic cyclic formation reactions can also be applied to the 3D building units. One of the PAF materials, PAF-2, was synthesized from the tetrahedral building unit, tetrakis(4-
cyanophenyl)methane.\(^{104}\) PAF-16 is a similar example and is built from the cyano cyclotrimerization of tetrahedral tetrakis-(4-cyanophenyl)silane.\(^{160}\) Sometimes task-specific cyano functionalized building units are not readily available because of the toxicity in the building unit synthesis procedure. Other aromatic cyclic formation reactions are desired for PAF syntheses. Cobalt-catalyzed acetylene trimerization would develop a benzene ring connection from the acetylene functionalized building units and is an attractive route for preparing porous frameworks.\(^{161-163}\)
2.4.4. Development of New Reactions for PAF Synthesis. Definitely more and more reactions are being exploited for the syntheses of porous frameworks. The continuous discovery of new reactions drives further development of PAFs with diverse connections and development of their chemistry. Currently used coupling reactions are commonly catalyzed by organometallic compounds, which are expensive and sensitive to air. The usage of these catalysts thus increases the cost of PAFs and limits their large-scale production. To target the practical application of PAFs, reducing their cost and increasing the production are necessary. To this end, some new reactions have been developed. Lewis acid catalyzed Friedel−Crafts alkylation reaction is able to connect the aromatic motifs via methylene under mild condition. Tetraphenylmethane and its amino and hydroxyl functionalized derivatives were polymerized by using the Friedel−Crafts alkylation reaction to give PAF-32, PAF-32- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2},\) and PAF-32-OH, respectively.\(^{164}\) All these PAFs showed the BET surface area from 1230 to \(1680\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Because of the existence of amino and hydroxyl groups in the frameworks, the PAF-32- \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and PAF-32-OH exhibited higher gas capture abilities than PAF-32. In a recently research on methane adsorption, benzene and 1,2-dichloroethane were polymerized by the Friedel−Crafts reaction in kilogram batches, and its current cost is about \(\mathbb{S}1\) per kilogram.\(^{49}\) Another similar reaction, which is also catalyzed by Lewis acid, namely Scholl reaction, is also a promising reaction to synthesize PAFs with low cost under mild conditions.\(^{165}\) Meanwhile, with the rapid development of the research field, reactions for synthesizing PAFs are not currently limited to those for carbon−carbon bond formation but also include the reactions of covalently connected heteroatoms such as N, O, and S. For example, reactions exploiting the nucleophilic substitution reaction of cyanuric chloride with amine,\(^{80,106}\) the azo formation reaction,\(^{166-168}\) polyimide formation,\(^{169-171}\) and others\(^{172-176}\) were reported for synthesizing porous organic frameworks. Some commonly used reactions for porous framework syntheses are summarized in Figure 10.
## 2.5. Structure Analysis for PAFs
2.5.1. Difficulties and Challenges. A significant factor influencing PAF technology is that although the PAF structures are based on topology-directed design and the simulation of their properties assumes a network of regularly repeated units, the irreversible nature of the connecting reaction means that the PAF structures are under kinetic control, which leads to PAFs being amorphous. Most PAF materials have a PXRD pattern indicating amorphousness. For example, because of the steric configuration of the tetrahedral core and the singular connectivity option of the cross coupling reaction, PAF-1 was initially recognized as a P2 structure, which is crystalline. However, later work by Trewin et al. compared a number of possible 4-connected frameworks with
amorphous ones and concluded that a three dimensionally disordered framework was the most energetically stable structure.\(^{142}\) The porosity arises from the contortion of connections between the rigid building units. The amorphous nature of most PAFs creates some difficulties in the understanding of their long-range structure and their structure−function correlation. Meanwhile, PAFs are different to discrete molecules or linear polymeric chains, in that they are extensively expanded frameworks and are insoluble in common solvents, meaning that characterizations carried out in the solution phase are unsuitable for analyzing PAFs. However, because of the simple composition and reactions for the synthesis of the aromatic frameworks, many other characterization methods can be applied to understand the local chemical environments within PAFs.
2.5.2. Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy. For characterization of the reactions involved in PAF syntheses, Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) is becoming one of the most important tools to monitor the synthesis progress and to provide key structural information. For example, for Ullmann coupling, the starting materials with C−Br bonds show their characteristic stretching vibration at around \(1050\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in the IR spectrum and the disappearance of
this vibration indicates completion of the PAF synthesis.\(^{79}\) Likewise, for the triazine formation reaction, the reduction of the \( \mathrm{C} \equiv \mathrm{N} \) stretching vibration at \( 2228~\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) accompanied by the emergence of a triazine ring at 1507 and \( 1352~\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) are clear evidence for a completed reaction.\(^{33}\) Additionally, FTIR is able to provide information on retention of desired functional groups in PAFs during synthesis and modification. For example, in the synthesis of the carboxyl-decorated PAF-26-COOH via the Sonogashira cross coupling reaction, the \( \mathrm{C} = \mathrm{O} \) stretching vibration at \( 1728~\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) and the \( \mathrm{O} - \mathrm{H} \) stretching vibration at \( 3431~\mathrm{cm}^{-1} \) are observed in the spectra of both the starting building unit and the as-synthesized PAF. This indicates that the carboxylic acid groups remain unaltered during the cross-coupling reaction.\(^{159}\) To date, most of the PAF characterizations by FTIR technology are empirical and based on comparisons of experimental data, but those data as input to FTIR and Raman simulations have in turn yielded an understanding of the structures and the interactions within the frameworks.\(^{177,178}\)
2.5.3. Nuclear Magnetic Resonance. Nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy is an adsorption spectroscopy technique for characterizing the chemical environments of certain nuclei. For insoluble PAF materials, the constrained spatial configurations of the measurable nuclei lead to dramatically broadened spectra. The dipolar−dipolar coupling in the solid phase can be averaged when the NMR sample is spinning at an angle of \( 54.74^{\circ} \) with respect to the direction of the external magnetic field. The so-called magic angle spinning nuclear magnetic resonance (MAS NMR) is another technology which can provide short-range ordering information about the insoluble porous frameworks in the solid state.\(^{179}\) The \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \) and \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) solid-state NMR studies are widely applied in studying the local chemical environments of the specific nuclei in PAFs because of the presence of aromatic motifs. For instance, in order to unravel the local structure of PAF-1, both \( ^{1}\mathrm{H} \) and \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) solid-state NMR of the sample were performed. The existence of two types of phenyl protons leads to the chemical shifts at 7.6 and \( 7.9~\mathrm{ppm} \), respectively. Meanwhile, in the \( ^{13}\mathrm{C} \) CP-MAS NMR spectrum, five distinguishable signals are assigned to the five different carbon atoms in the proposed PAF-1 structure.\(^{31}\) Moreover, solid-state NMR and related technologies also provide evidence of the framework dynamics in PAFs. The deuterium spin−echo NMR measurement indicates the intensely rotating of the phenyl motifs in PAFs, which makes the PAF framework a molecular rotor with guest-molecule modulated rotational frequency.\(^{180}\)
2.5.4. Thermogravimetric Analysis and Elemental Analysis. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) is the method for measuring the weight change of the sample when combustion occurs. The weight changes of a porous framework at different temperatures can be attributed to many causes such as the removal of the guest molecules from the framework, decomposition of the organic framework, and oxidation of the residual metals from catalysts in the coupling reactions. TG analysis shows the PAF-1 start to loss its weight until \( 520~^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) under air, which is due to the oxidation and decomposition of the framework at high temperature. Moreover, TGA results provide definite evidence about the metal residues in the highly porous framework. The weight residue of PAF-1 sample is almost 0 at the temperature above \( 600~^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), indicating the framework is entirely organic and no metal catalyst is existed in the framework. To provide qualitative and quantitative analysis of the PAF compositional information, characterizations can be
carried out such as CHNS analysis, energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) and inductively coupled plasma spectroscopy (ICP).
2.5.5. Porosity Measurement. The essential structural feature of PAFs is their porosity, especially at the microporous scale (less than \( 2~\mathrm{nm} \)), which originates from the construction of building units at molecular level. It is widely recognized that gas adsorption is the most commonly used technique to estimate their surface area, pore volume fraction, and pore size distribution. Before the measurement, degassing is mandatory in order to clean the surface and empty the framework. For PAF materials, degassing is carried out by heating the sample under vacuum to remove the solvent and any volatile guests within the framework. The heating temperature is carefully determined to avoid burning the organic framework. Inorganic catalysts and unreacted building units usually cannot be removed by methods such as heating and therefore require certain pretreatments. For inorganic catalysts, the PAF sample usually needs to be washed by an acid or base solution. The exceptional framework stability of PAFs usually renders the samples tolerant to harsh chemical treatments unless certain environmentally sensitive functional groups were introduced. Unreacted building units are trapped in the framework and need to be thoroughly removed by Soxhlet extraction.
Gas adsorption experiments are carried out by measuring the adsorption−desorption isotherm of the degassed sample at a constant temperature. The isotherm represents the equilibrium adsorption amount of an adsorptive in terms of its volume at standard temperature and pressure (STP) as a function of the relative pressure \( P/P_{0} \). \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) is the most commonly used adsorptive gas as probe molecules. But \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) is a nonspherical molecule with a low critical temperature \( (77~\mathrm{K}) \), and therefore it has difficulty in diffusing into microporous materials with a pore diameter under \( 0.7~\mathrm{nm} \), and it takes longer to equilibrate within a carbon-based porous framework. For the measurement of the narrow microporosity, \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) adsorption at 273 or \( 298~\mathrm{K} \) is a suitable alternative to \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) adsorption at \( 77~\mathrm{K} \).\(^{181}\) Therefore, although the most commonly used adsorptive is presently nitrogen, on many occasions other gases such as carbon dioxide have been applied for porosity assessment of PAFs.\(^{109,127}\)
It is well understood that the shape of the isotherms indicates the structures of the porous materials. The rapid gas uptake observed at relative low \( P/P_{0} \), which is usually below 0.1, can be attributed to micropore filling. The resulting isotherm is initially convex with respect to the gas uptake axis, followed by a plateau of adsorption with increasing relative pressure. This curve shape is classified as a type I adsorption by IUPAC. The type I isotherm is regarded as evidence for the existence of microporous frameworks. In regard to PAF materials, that are synthesized via kinetic control, the particle sizes are usually small and the interparticulate spaces significantly affect the gas uptake, leading to increase in gas uptake with increasing relative pressure. If the framework possesses mesopores, then an uptake assigned to capillary condensation at medium relative pressure with a hysteresis loop can be observed. This curve shape is thus considered indicative of a mesoporous framework.
Porous parameters, such as the surface area, pore volume, and pore size distributions, are evaluated from the data present in adsorption isotherms based on different models. The Langmuir method assumes that monolayer adsorption of the adsorptive occurs, therefore it usually gives an estimate of the
surface area for porous materials higher than it does through the Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) method. The BET method is an improvement on the Langmuir model but with the assumption that the interactions within the adsorbed gas are about the same as that between the gas and the adsorbent, therefore multiple layer adsorption occurs. It has to be emphasized that at present no single theory can provide exacting solutions for all the targets and different models need to be selected with caution. To obtain meaningful surface area information, Rouquerol plots are required to determine the linear range of the BET data for PAF materials.182 Nonlocal density functional theory (NLDFT)183 and quenched solid density functional theory (QSDFT)184 models based on computational procedures are gradually replacing the earlier methods such as Barrett−Joyner−Halenda (BJH) theory. They provide a number of models based on distinct adsorbates such as active carbon, zeolites, and oxides, to assess the pore size distribution of the sample. The results are reasonable and reliable if an appropriate model was initially selected. Therefore, pore size results from all possible models should be compared while also taking into account chemical rationality.185
## 3. GENERAL STRATEGIES AND EXAMPLES FOR PAF FUNCTIONALIZATION
PAFs with highly accessible surface and pores at the molecular scale are considered a platform for a range of functionalities. The desired properties for advanced materials for specific applications can be obtained by task-specific functionalization for the PAFs. Clearly, PAFs possess advantages over other porous frameworks through functional modification. Primarily, as we have demonstrated in the last section, PAFs are constructed from rigid building units and their connections made by specific reactions. Therefore, the functionalities of PAF materials can be introduced through the task-specific designed building units themselves or from the connecting groups produced during prenetwork-synthesis reactions (presynthesis). A strong complement to this avenue arises from the existence of aromatic motifs within the framework and the unexceptional chemical stability of PAFs: the frameworks can be readily modified with desired functionalities through well-established reactions ("postsynthesis"). Both strategies are well developed and together build up a broad and reliable repertoire for the advanced applications of PAFs. Also, more complex functionality can be achieved by combining both the strategies. In conclusion, the unique characteristics of PAFs have led to three general strategies for task-specific functionalization: direct synthesis from predesigned building units, postsynthetic modification, and direct synthesis of PAFs with anchored sites for further post modification to adjust the chemistry of the PAFs. The strategies are illustrated with some examples below and shown in Figure 11.
## 3.1. Direct Synthesis
Direct synthesis of functional PAFs relies on the design and synthesis of functionalized building units. Building units with various functional groups such as amine, carboxylate, hydroxyl, and nitro groups can tune the chemistry of the PAF framework.105,129,159,190 For direct synthesis of functional PAFs from building units with specific functionality, the synthetic reaction should be carefully selected as these functional groups have a strong impact on the synthetic
reactions apart from affecting the porosity of the PAFs. For example, Yamamoto-type Ullmann coupling is sensitive to the reacting conditions and is incompatible with the presence of the nitro group. A nitro-functionalized PAF material \(\left(\mathbf{NO}_{2}\right.\) - PAF-1) can be synthesized from the nitro-containing building units via a modified copper-mediated Ullmann coupling.190 Solid-state NMR, FTIR, and elemental analysis provided evidence for the well-defined nitro functionalization of PAFs by direct synthesis. PAFs with amine, carboxylate, or hydroxyl functional groups were synthesized via the Sonogashira− Hagihara cross-coupling reaction, which is more tolerant of the chemistry of the reagents than is the Ni catalyzed Ullmann reaction.129,159 For instance, PAF-26 is a carboxyl functionalized PAF material, using dibromobenzoic acid as one of the building units via a Sonogashira−Hagihara cross-coupling reaction. The carboxyl functional groups are retained during the synthetic process. Using the postmetalation approach, light metal elements, including Li, Na, K, and \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g},\) are located within the PAF-26 framework. The amount of metal loading, determined empirically, suggested that almost \(85\%\) of the carboxyl groups are replaced by metal ions, indicating high binding efficiency of the carboxyl-functionalized PAFs.159
Compared to the post synthetic modification, direct synthesis ensures a very definitive location and stoichiometry for the functional groups. For instance, Nguyen et al. reported a de novo synthetic strategy191 by replacing the tetrahedral building units used in PAF-1 with the methyl-, methanol,- or phthalimide-functionalized tetrakis(4-bromophenyl) methane. Functionalized building units are used for the synthesis of PAFs with retention of their functional groups. In addition, the phthalimide functionalized PAF was treated through a postsynthesis deprotection process and an amine-functionalized PAF was obtained. The authors noted that compared with the postsynthetic modification where the inhomogeneity of functional groups may occur due to the lower functionality of the inner part of the PAF particles than of the outer part, the de novo approach may cause even distribution of the functional groups (Figure 12). The building unit for PAF-1, tetrakis(4-bromophenyl)methane, has been decorated with a cationic imidazolium group. The designed cationic building unit has been used in synthesis via the Ullmann coupling reaction to give an ionic PAF-1 (iPAF-1). Compared with
PAF-1, the surface area of iPAF-1 is relatively low due to the presence of the bulky and heavy imidazolium bromide. But with large amounts of the imidazolium motifs in the framework, there are suitable active sites for selective adsorption of polar organic compounds. The direct synthesis from designed building units ensures a stoichiometric and homogeneous distribution of the desired functional groups. On the basis of the homogeneous structure, it is possible to simulate and understand the adsorbing procedure in amorphous structures by coarse-graining and molecular dynamics.143
In addition to the building units with modified functional groups, many molecules possessing intrinsic properties are deployed as building units for functional PAFs. A series of molecules containing a 4-diketopyrrolo[3,4-c]-pyrrole (DPP) fluorescent motif were incorporated into a porous framework as building units. The framework structure spatially isolates the fluorescent DPPs and thereby avoids quenching caused by aggregation. Consequently, the obtained porous polymer networks with fluorescent dyes as building units exhibit enhanced solid-state fluorescence. Porous frameworks with fluorescence properties provide space for confinement of coumarin dyes. The host−guest composite forms a donor−acceptor system with outstanding energy transfer efficiency, and it can be employed as a light-harvesting array.192
Heteroatoms have strong electronic effects on adjacent aromatic motifs and regulate the framework's functionalities through their optical or electric properties. The modification can be induced by the functionalized building units. An electron-rich PAF has been synthesized from the triangular building unit triphenylamine, which contains a central nitrogen atom and three electron-rich phenyl groups.186 A PAF with a central phosphorus atom has been prepared from the tris(4-chlorophenyl)phosphine building units via the Ullmann coupling reaction. The oxidation state of the central P atoms can be tuned by a simple chemical redox reaction.187,188
A series of PAFs based on porphyrin and its derivatives as building units has been synthesized. The emphasis for the synthesis work is the unique electronic structure of porphyrin that is likely to impart specific electronic, photonic, or catalytic properties to the porous frameworks. Functional PAFs with building units of porphyrin or pyrene exhibit multiple
functions including \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture, \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\) adsorption, and singlet oxygen generation.193
The synergistic effect in PAFs occurs when the systematic integration of two or more functional groups enables a specific application. or in some cases, specific functions are achieved by the coordination of multiple fragments. The spatial regularity of the functional groups or fragments is vital for synergy. To avoid random modification of the porous frameworks with multiple functional groups, a reliable strategy is to design the building units with synergistic functional motifs and directly synthesize the PAFs. An example is the synthesis of molecularly imprinted PAFs. Multiple functional fragments are preassembled to form molecular imprinted building units that are then used in direct synthesis of PAFs, which acquire the desired functions. Molecularly imprinted PAFs are particularly efficient for applications such as metal capture194,195 and catalysis.148
## 3.2. Postsynthetic Modification
Direct synthesis of functional PAFs is limited when the synthetic reaction is incompatible with the functional groups on the building unit. Compared with direct synthesis, postsynthetic modification of PAFs is more pervasive to a wide range of functionalities, owing to the exceptional stability of PAFs and the amenability of the aromatic motifs in the framework.
Sulfonate acid is a strong Brønsted acid. Functionalizing PAFs with the sulfonate group may improve the framework acidity and create active sites for acid-catalyzed reactions. Sulfonate-grafted PAFs, from postsynthetic modification, are widely reported in the literature. A sulfonate-functionalized PAF was first reported by Zhou et al.: PPN-6, which is synthesized from the same building units and reaction as for PAF-1, was treated by chlorosulfonic acid in dichloromethane to give PPN-6-SO3H, with an estimated ratio of benzene rings to sulfonate group of 2 (Figure 13).40 The high stability of
PAFs ensures they are tolerant to the extreme environmental conditions of strong acidity and high temperature. Two PAFs that were synthesized through the Pd-catalyzed Suzuki−Miyaura coupling reactions were treated at \(160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in 98 wt % sulfuric acid. The sulfonate contents were assessed by titration, indicating that over 65% of the benzene rings in the PAFs were successfully sulfonated. That sulfonate content is higher than in commercially available acidic resin. Additionally, the superior catalytic performance in the esterification reaction of the synthesized solid acid indicated that it was more acidic than the acidic resins.196
Framework modification for specific functionalities can be achieved by multistep reactions. Amine-functionalized PAFs can be obtained from nitro-PAFs, which are obtained by nitric
acid treatment of PAFs. Subsequent reduction of nitro groups in the framework yields amine-functionalized PAFs.125,197 Amine functionality not only provides basicity and electron donating sites to alter the chemistry of the PAF frameworks but also plays the role of anchor sites to bind other functional groups. Molecules with specific functionalities such as a fluorescent chromophore or catalytic activity were attached to PAF frameworks through reactions with amine.198,199 Benzyl chloride \(\left(-\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\right)\) is another anchoring site that can be attached to PAFs by post chloromethylated modification. Because of its high reactivity, functional groups such as alkylamine, \(N\!\cdot\) -heterocyclic carbene and metal coordinating groups were also bound with the benzyl chloride for framework modification.200−203
## 3.3. Post Modification of PAFs with Preanchored Sites
Direct synthesis of functional PAFs causes the functionality in the framework to be distributed homogeneously, but the selection of the functional groups is limited by the coupling reactions. In contrast, postsynthetic modification is able to introduce a wide range of functional groups, however, patchy distribution of the functional groups in the framework occurs, and "dead space" is formed if the pores on the outside surface are blocked by the functional groups. Combining the two strategies of direct synthesis with anchoring sites and postmodification for specific functionality at the sites, a new synthetic method has been developed to overcome the disadvantages of each strategy. In this method, the preanchored sites are less reactive to the coupling reaction, thus it will not affect the PAF synthesis. Meanwhile, the preanchored sites are readily modified by post treatment. The preanchored sites in the as-synthesized PAFs are converted to desired functionalities. The so-called "anchor installation" method was reported for synthesis of alkylamine-functionalized porous polymer networks for carbon capture: A porous polymeric network (PPN-200) was synthesized from the building units with methyl group as preanchored sites. Then the methyl group was converted to methyl bromide group and further substituted by alkylamines. The PPN-200-amine from anchor installation would have the homogeneously distributed alkylamine groups in its framework. Because of the steric hindrance of the alkylamine groups in the framework, the PPN-200-amine shows obvious decrease of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptake but its \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture capacity is up to 8.3 wt \(\%^{204}\) A functional molecule, metallo-porphyrin, which is a triplet sensitizer, is covalently bonded to an emitting PAF (ePAF) with a hydroxyl group as the anchor site. The resultant sensitizer-integrated ePAF (i-ePAF), with the fluorescent component incorporated in the porous framework without segregation or aggregation, ensures that individual upconverters are distributed in a stable framework for photoluminescent applications.205 The postsynthetic modification at the preanchored sites ensures the homogeneity of the functional sites in PAF frameworks, therefore it is considered as an important functionalizing strategy.
## 3.4. PAFs with Charged Frameworks
Porous materials with charged frameworks are the subject of vigorous research, not only because they diversify PAF structures but they also provide many more unique features than the neutral frameworks.206 Direct synthesis of a series of charged PAFs (PAF-23, PAF-24, and PAF-25) has been reported, using lithium tetrakis(4-iodophenyl)borate as the tetrahedral building units and coupling with different alkyne
building units. The ionic motif in the framework is an efficient adsorption site for iodine, and the charged frameworks therefore have potential for radioactive element capture (Figure 14).109 PAFs with charged frameworks can also be
synthesized using reactions that produce ionic motifs. Through the reaction of pyridinium salt formation, PAF-50 with a cationic framework and \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) as counterions were synthesized. The charged framework with negative counterions allows tuning of the pore size of PAFs via ion exchange. The \(\mathrm{Cl^{-}}\) in the PAF-50 framework exchanged with other halogen anions, resulting in the pore sizes varying in the range \(3.4{-}7\mathrm{~\AA~}\) . The controllable pore sizes at the Ågstrom scale allows molecular sieving effect for use in separation of gas mixtures with multiple components.207
A charged framework synthesized from the post synthetic modification of PAF-1 was reported by Ma et al. The grafting of ammonium motifs on PAF-1 led to PAF-based ion exchange materials suitable for capturing noble metals in trace amounts, adsorption of model ions from radioactive waste, and organic dye capture by the size-selective effect. The charged PAF-1 is a promising ion exchange material due to its high stability, fast ion exchange rate, high ion exchange capacity, and nonswelling character of the materials without functional site entrainment.208
## 3.5. Wettability and Polarity of PAF Frameworks
PAFs are typically hydrophobic due to the presence of benzene rings and carbon−carbon covalent linkages. The hydrophobicity contributes to the adsorption of more aromatic organic compounds such as benzene and toluene. For example, PAF-19 and PAF-20 contain pyrene-based building units and exhibit high uptake of benzene and methanol at \(298\,\mathrm{~K},\) but meanwhile their water adsorption is low, indicating the hydrophobicity of the framework.118 However, the hydrophobicity has a negative effect on applications in aqueous solutions or in high humidity. For example, removal of
contaminants from water requires high wettability of the adsorbents. Membranes for desalination also need a hydrophilic channel for permeation of water. For this application, the wettability and polarity of the PAF pores have to be tuned. Polar functional groups can be introduced into the PAF framework, by either direct synthesis or postsynthetic modification. A comparison of the porous polarity and surface wettability of PAFs was conducted for three PAFs with similar skeletons but different functional groups. The results of surface-contact angle measurements and water adsorption indicated very distinct effects from the pore modification. Hydrophilic PAFs with various pore polarities are particularly suited for removal of the corresponding antibiotics from water.\[189\]
## 4. STATE-OF-THE-ART OF PAF APPLICATIONS
PAFs with multifarious porosities have been synthesized from various building units via different reactions. Their framework chemistry is either derived from the building units or is tuned by functionalization. The porosity and chemistry of PAFs together with their highly amendable frameworks render them promising materials for a diverse range of applications, from the classical applications of porous materials such as adsorption, ion exchange, and catalysis to advanced applications in electrochemistry and stimuli-responsive materials. In this section, applications based on PAF materials will be summarized and discussed.
## 4.1. Gas Adsorption
Compared with denser solids, porous materials provide numerous voids in which to accommodate gas molecules. This intrinsic property is not only used to physicochemically characterize the surface features of porous solids, but it also makes them important absorbents in many industrial processes. Because of the current worldwide energy and environmental crises, gas storage and separation are prominent research topics. The former includes storage of energy supply gases such as hydrogen and methane. Hydrogen \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\right)\) is a promising replacement for fossil fuels because if derived from \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) using renewable energy then it too is a renewal resource. Regardless of its derivation it has a cleaner combustion product (viz. \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\)) than fossil fuels, and similarly for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), but depending on its source. Both have high energy densities (142 \(\mathrm{kJ\,g^{-1}}\) for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(55.7\,\mathrm{kJ\,g^{-1}}\) for \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\)) compared with gasoline \((46.4\,\mathrm{kJ\,g^{-1}})\). For in situ vehicular use, the main technological hurdle is how to improve the capacity of storage and delivery for these energy supply gases. Porous absorbents have significant advantages for gas storage and capture compared with conventional methods such as cryogenic storage, storage via high-pressure gas tanks, and chemical adsorption. The adsorptive processes in the porous absorbents have relatively low cost, reduced energy consumption, and safe operation. Reducing the concentration of greenhouse gases in the atmosphere from anthropogenic emissions has attracted extensive attention in recent years, and this is where gas separation and adsorption technology can play a part. The successful implementation of selective \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture from postcombustion flue gas relies on effective separation of a gas mixture. Other gas separation processes include selective separation of hydrocarbon mixtures and the capture of trace amounts of toxic gas from the environment. This is beneficial to energy conservation and a cleaner environment. A fundamental property of PAFs is that many parameters, such
as density of active sites, surface area, and pore size, influence both gas adsorption and selective capture in a similar fashion. Therefore, many studies have measured the adsorptive performances of multiple gases concurrently.\[39,45,209,210\]
Two main targets in the design and synthesis of porous absorbents are generally a higher gas holding capacity and a stronger binding between the absorbent and one component of the gas mixture. Porous adsorbents, especially MOFs, have been very successful at gas storage and adsorptive separation of gas mixtures. This is due to their high surface area, unsaturated metal sites, and broad diversity of framework functionality. Similarly, PAFs possess highly porous frameworks with features including adsorbing active sites and polarity and adjustability of these features. Moreover, compared with the majority of MOFs, the thermal and chemical stability of PAFs are much greater because their covalent linkages are stronger than the coordinating bonds in MOFs. The characteristics of PAFs mean they are promising absorbers for gases important in industrial processes. The first reported PAF, PAF-1, exhibited an excess hydrogen uptake of 7.0 wt % at 48 bar and 77K and a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake of \(1300\,\mathrm{mg\,g^{-1}}\) at 40 bar and \(298\,\mathrm{K}\), both being the highest for porous materials at the time. PAFs with various structures and functionalities have been screened, designed, and synthesized with the aim of improving gas uptake capacity and binding strength.
4.1.1. Hydrogen Storage. Safety concerns, low uptake efficiency, and high cost are currently the main hurdles for storage and delivery of hydrogen for energy production. The U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) has set a specific target to reduce the current vehicular consumption of fossil fuels: the gravimetric capacity of the hydrogen storage system must reach 5.5 wt % and \(40\,\mathrm{g\,L^{-1}}\) in volumetric capacity by 2025.\[211\]
Hydrogen storage by physical adsorption in porous absorbents has outstanding advantages in terms of the energy penalty, safety, operational complexity, and apparatus complexity when compared with other technologies such as liquid cryogenic storage, high-pressure gas tanks, and chemical bond formation in metal hydrides (chemisorption). Using porous absorbents for hydrogen storage is therefore a promising technology in which great progress has already been made, especially by MOFs.\[17,212,213\] Although \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage capacities that exceed the DOE target have been achieved by a wide range of materials, including porous carbon,\[214\] MOFs,\[15−217\] and COFs,\[218,219\]
there is room for improvement in practicalities, such as stability, recyclability, and cost. Noteworthy also is that most of the current reports for absorbents are based on laboratory-scale systems, but their capacities would decrease if the weight of the device and auxiliary equipment was included. Therefore, more research into \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) physisorption storage capacity is necessary. Moreover, hydrogen absorbents usually exhibit relative low enthalpy of adsorption \(\left(\Delta H_{\mathrm{ads}}\right)\) or an isosteric heat of adsorption \(\left(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\right)\), with the absolute value in the range \(1-10\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}\). This value suggests a weak interaction between \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and the absorbent's surface. Hydrogen uptake by absorbents is most often reported at cryogenic temperatures of \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) or \(87\,\mathrm{K}\) and that adsorbate capacity dramatically decreases at near-ambient conditions due to increased thermal motion of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and thus a consequently lower effectivity of the binding sites, resulting in less bound \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\). The loss of absorbance due to increased thermal motion could be attenuated with a binding site that bonds more strongly to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\). Considering these drawbacks, research into \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage currently has two main objectives: to increase the hydrogen storage capacity and to
enhance the binding strength of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) onto porous adsorbents. In general, PAFs exhibit superior physiochemical stability to MOFs due to the strength disparity between covalent bonds and coordination bonds. The exceptional stability of PAFs not only renders them industrially practical but also provides diverse possibilities for framework modification. Meanwhile, PAFs possess comparable surface area and porosity to MOFs. Therefore, investigations into PAFs as promising \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) absorbents have been extensively conducted.
Experimentally, the hydrogen storage capacities in PAFs are measured either at ambient or high pressure (40−60 bar) and at 77 or \(87\,\mathrm{K}\). Although the volumetric capacity is as important as the gravimetric capacity, it may be even more important to applications in onboard systems.220 Research into the volumetric capacity of PAFs is still limited by a lack of data. The gravimetric capacities of a range of PAFs under high and ambient pressure are summarized in Table 2.
Computational simulation assists in the design and screening of nanoporous materials for hydrogen storage.228,229 Multiscale simulation methods combining first-principles calculations and grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulation has been used to forecast the hydrogen storage capacities of several PAFs with diamondoid structures. The results indicate a gravimetric capacity up to 6.53 wt \(\%\) at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) and 100 bar is achievable if the \(\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{C}\) bond in the diamondoid structure is replaced by four phenyl rings.94 Similarly, other nonfunctionalized PAF structures with different topologies,97 enlarged aromatic motifs,95 or ethynyl linkages98 were proposed and screened with the aim of looking for absorbents with a high \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage capacity. The simulations reveal the contribution of surface area to the gravimetric capacity.
PAF materials with ultrahigh surface areas have been prepared in order to meet the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage capacity target. The correlations between gravimetric capacity for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and BET surface area at elevated pressure and ambient pressure are shown in Figures 15 and 16, respectively, for a comprehensive range of PAFs. For instance, at high pressure (48−60 bar) and at 77K, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage capacities range from 4.2 wt \(\%\) for PAF-4, with a BET surface area of \(2246\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), to 7.0 wt \(\%\) at 77K for PAF-1, with a BET surface area of \(5600\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\); PPN-3 with BET surface area of \(2840\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) has a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake of 4.28 wt \(\%\) at 42 bar and \(77\,\mathrm{K}\),39 but at even higher pressure (80 bar) and at 77K PPN-4 with the highest reported BET surface area, of \(6461\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), has a storage capacity of 8.34 wt \(\%\) .107 Some results have exceeded the DOE target for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) gravimetric capacity. At high pressure, a better linear correlation is observed compared with at ambient pressure: \(\mathrm{R}^{2}=71\%\), \(P<0.0001\) in Figure 15; compared with \(\mathrm{R}^{2}=26\%\), \(P<0.002\) in Figure 16, respectively. Therefore, it could be concluded that at high pressure differences in surface area have greater significance on hydrogen uptake. This in accordance with
the empirical Chahine's rule, which suggests a positive linear correlation between the excess gravimetric capacity of a wide range of porous absorbents and their surface areas.230 At ambient pressure, due to the absence of high pressure as the driving force for the gas adsorption, the correlation between gravimetric capacity and surface area of the absorbents decreases; points diverge more from the line of best fit (Figure 16). The higher divergence indicates that although \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capacity is still strongly related to the surface area of absorbent, other aspects are having greater influence on \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage. Meanwhile, the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capacities of these absorbents (shown in Figure 16), as well as of other PAFs at ambient pressure, are all below the DOE target. As shown in Figure 16, the data points distinctly above the line of best fit but close to the ordinate axis possess a reasonable \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake value but relatively low surface area.
These PAFs may attract particular attention because the hydrogen storage capacities could be further improved by altering their surface areas.
The hydrogen binding strength is another important factor for hydrogen storage, apart from absorption capacity. Most PAFs have low isosteric heats of adsorption. This gives a satisfactory \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake at \(77\;\mathrm{~K},\) but it would dramatically decrease with increasing temperature. A correlation between the optimal temperature \(\left(T_{\mathrm{opt}}\right)\) and adsorption enthalpy \(\Delta H_{\mathrm{opt}}^{\mathrm{o}}\) under \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) charge/discharge pressures \(\left(P_{1}/P_{2}\right)\) is given by
\[
T_{\mathrm{opt}}^{\mathrm{o}}=\frac{\Delta H_{\mathrm{opt}}^{\mathrm{o}}}{[\Delta S^{\circ}+(R/2)\ln(P_{2}/P_{0}^{2})]}
\]
where \(\Delta S^{\circ}\) is the entropy change of about \(-8\mathrm{R}\) for hydrogen adsorption at standard pressure.231 If the charge and discharge pressures for a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage facility are fixed at 30/1.5 bar, then at \(298~\mathrm{K}\) the optimal adsorption enthalpy should be \(-15.1\ \mathrm{kJ}\) \(\mathrm{mol^{-1}}\) . A recent review suggests that optimal hydrogen binding energies should be within 10 to \(25\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}\) ,72 which means that for PAFs it has to be increased above their current values. The nature of the PAF framework provides diverse possibilities for functionalization. Many strategies which have proven effective in increasing the hydrogen binding strength for MOFs, such as optimizing porous structures,232 introducing unsaturated metal sites,233 impregnating,234 and alkali element doping,235 have been applied to PAFs, either experimentally or computationally.
The porous volume and pore size influence the uptake capacity and binding strength because for smaller pores the hydrogen molecules can be attracted from multiple sides of the pore simultaneously. This suggests that for hydrogen, micropores are needed for higher heats of adsorption, and a pore size below \(1\ \mathrm{nm}\) is ideal.72,76 A nearly linear correlation between the absolute value of heat/enthalpy of adsorption and pore size of selected PAFs (as shown in Figure 17) also evidence that
micropores in PAFs positively contribute to hydrogen storage. As an example, a series of porous polymer networks (PPN-1, PPN-2, and PPN-3) have been synthesized from tetrahedral building units via the oxidative Eglinton coupling or Yamamoto-type Ullmann coupling reaction. Because of strong interactions and a lower steric hindrance core of the building units, PPN-1 is believed to have an interpenetrating structure and it exhibits more micropores less than \(1\ \mathrm{nm}\) diameter than do PPN-2 and PPN-3. This gives PPN-1 the highest heat of
adsorption among these three porous polymer networks (7.59, 6.89, and \(5.51\ \mathrm{~kJ~}\ \mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) for PPN-1, PPN-2, and PPN-3, respectively).39 Besides synthesis-directed porosity control, the pore size and pore volume of PAFs are also reduced by carbonization, during which the cavities decrease with higher temperatures. For example, when heating PAF-1 at different temperatures, the surface area, pore volumes, and pore sizes are substantially reduced. Remarkably, PAF-1 450, after heating to \(650\ ^{\circ}{\cal C},\) has a pore volume of \(0.53\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}\) and a pore size of 1 nm diameter, and its heat of adsorption is \(8.2\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}\) , the highest among all the reported carbonized PAFs. The hydrogen capacity increases from 1.66 wt \(\%\) for PAF-1 to 1.93 wt \(\%\) for carbonized PAF-1.232 PAFs that have been activated by potassium hydroxide and then carbonized partially retain their high surface area and therefore also their hydrogen gravimetric capacity, up to 3.06 wt \(\%\) at ambient pressure.223
The hydrogen binding energy for MOFs can be increased by lithium doping.237,238 Similarly, computational simulation and experimental investigations of Li-doping in PAFs have also been implemented. A PAF modified with a lithium tetrazolide group (PAF-4) has been proposed and its hydrogen uptake simulated. Because of the existence of multiple binding sites, the hydrogen absorbance of PAF-4 is forecast to be exceptional, with a gravimetric capacity of 20.7 wt \(\%\) and a volumetric capacity of \(68.6\mathrm{~g~L^{-1}}\) (at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) and 100 bar) and high binding energy up to \(14.5\;\;\mathrm{kJ}\;\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) .239 Experimental investigations show several examples of increased hydrogen binding energy in PAFs with various Li-doping sites. Reduction of a PAF-1 framework by lithiation leads to a delocalized anionic charge within the framework plus the incorporation of aromatic lithium-based complexes (Figure 18). A PAF-1
structure with \(5\%\) Li has a \(\Delta H_{\mathrm{ads}}\) of \(9\,\mathrm{\kJ\,\mol^{-1}}\) , which is almost double that of the original PAF-1 \(\Delta H_{\mathrm{ads}}\) is \(4.6\,\mathrm{~kJ}\) \(\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.\) ), and its hydrogen capacity at 77K and ambient pressure is increased by \(22\%\) .221 Similarly, PAF-18-OLi with a lithium phenoxide functional group has a heat of absorption of \(7.6\;\mathrm{kJ}\) \(\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) .
Inspired by the unsaturated metal sites in MOFs, which are particularly successful at increasing the hydrogen binding strength, PAFs with unsaturated metal sites have also been proposed and made. By introducing the 1,2,4,5-benzenetetroxide motif into a diamondoid PAF structure, functional PAFs incorporating metal cations such as \(\mathrm{Mg}\) and Ca have been simulated. These metal PAFs (PAF-Mg and PAF-Ca) were forecast to possess relatively low heats of adsorption but a large number of binding sites (18 for PAF-Mg and 22 for PAF-Ca). Therefore, their gravimetric capacity, as predicted, is improved to 6.8 wt \(\%\) for PAF-Mg and 6.4 wt \(\%\) for PAF-Ca. The simulation of the materials with metal sites and polar motifs inspires a promising approach to high hydrogen capacity by
designing PAFs with a large number of binding sites and relatively low binding energies.\(^{240}\) Polyhedral oligomeric silsesquioxane (POSS) frameworks connected via boron-modified aromatic rings as linkers have been simulated. With transition metals such as Sc and Ti incorporated within the framework, the Kubas interaction\(^{241}\) between a transition metal and hydrogen enhances hydrogen adsorption. The simulation predicts that a POSS framework with metal doping would meet both the gravimetric and volumetric capacity target set by the DOE.\(^{242}\)
It has been shown empirically that hydrogen binding strength is improved in electron-rich frameworks. JUC-Z2 is constructed entirely from electron-rich triphenylamine building units and possesses a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) heat of adsorption of \(6.8\,\mathrm{~kJ}\) \(\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\).\(^{186}\) In contrast, the PAF-1 constructed entirely from tetraphenylmethane and JUC-Z7, which is constructed from an \(80/20\%\) mixture of tetraphenylmethane/triphenylamine possess low heats of adsorption of 5.35 and \(5.65\;\;\mathrm{kJ}\;\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\), respectively.\(^{225}\) As N provides an extra electron pair which can enrich the electron density of aromatic rings, other N-containing frameworks have also been created. PAF-30 was synthesized by \(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{N}}2\) nucleophilic substitution between tetra-(4-anilyl)-methane and cyanuric chloride and possesses multiple N sites including amine and triazine rings. The pore size of PAF-30 is between 0.33 and \(0.35\;\mathrm{nm}\). Because of its multiple N sites and narrow pore sizes, it has a \(\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{st}}\) of \(19.6\;\mathrm{kJ}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\).\(^{106}\) It is a rare example of a porous organic material with hydrogen heat of adsorption in the range \(15{-}20\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}\).
The N-containing PAFs have proven that the incorporation of heterogeneous atoms in the frameworks enhances interaction with hydrogen. A much wider variety of chemical units has been presented. Initial isosteric heats of adsorption as high as \(8.0\;\;\mathrm{kJ}\;\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) were observed in the silsesquioxane framework of PSN-2, which is a polyorganosiloxane network with ACO topology.\(^{85}\) Replacement of the methyl group found in PPN-13 with fluorine to make PPN-12 yields a heat of adsorption of \(8.1\ \mathrm{kJ\ mol^{-1}}\), which is \(40\%\) higher than that for PPN-13.\(^{227}\) The phosphine- and phosphine-oxide-centered JUC-Z4 and JUC-Z5 exhibit \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) heats of adoption of 8.0 and \(8.1\;\;\mathrm{kJ}\;\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\), respectively, although their surface areas are below \(1000\;\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which may give a low \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake capacity.\(^{187}\)
With regard to the volumetric capacity, however, there seems to be a trade-off correlation with the high surface area for MOFs and other porous absorbents.\(^{243}\) The large porous volume of high-surface-area PAFs usually leads to a relatively low volumetric \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capacity. Because of the volumetric capacity having more impact than gravimetric capacity on the driving range of fuel cell vehicles,\(^{220}\) it is also necessary to improve the volumetric \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) capacity of PAFs. One strategy is to create extra sites on the adsorbing surface to improve the utilization of the voids. Molecular simulation of lithium-decorated fullerenes \(\left(\mathrm{Li}_{6}\mathrm{C}_{60}\right)\) in PAF-303 and PAF-304 forecasted that their volumetric \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) uptake would increase from 12 to \(44\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\) after the \(\mathrm{Li}_{6}\mathrm{C}_{60}\) is incorporated in PAF-304.\(^{244}\) That report reaffirmed the probability of achieving high volumetric hydrogen uptake by functionalized PAFs.
Most of the current studies into hydrogen storage by PAFs are based on solid powders. In practice, their processability and mechanical properties are important and consequently investigation into PAF based composites has been undertaken, nominally by embedding PAF-1 particulates into PIM-1. Self-supporting films with different gravimetric proportions of PAF-1/PIM-1 were prepared with a maximum PAF-1 doping content of \(37.5\%\). Their hydrogen uptake contents increased with increasing PAF-1 content. The mechanical properties of the PAF-based composites were also investigated. The PIM's ultimate tensile stress and strain falls with an increasing proportion of PAF-1, but the elastic properties are almost unchanged.\(^{245}\) Investigation into the processability of PAF composites represents an important step toward practical applications.
