Compared with the benchmark data of any other porous solids for hydrogen storage,\(^{17,246}\) the corresponding performance for PAFs still needs further improvement by increasing their \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding enthalpies and increasing their volumetric capacities. To increase the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) binding enthalpy, targeted synthesis of PAFs with pore size below \(1\ \mathrm{nm}\) and more micropores is desired. This may be achieved via a careful selection of the building units or through the framework interpenetration (see section 2.3.2 and 2.3.3). Meanwhile, metal doping or incorporating heteroatoms into the framework are also effective routes to a better \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) adsorbent. Effective binding sites for metal loading or synthetic precursors with heteroatoms can be incorporated by direct synthesis or post synthetic modification. For the PAFs with high volumetric capacity, the framework density and the effective adsorbing sites should be well balanced.
4.1.2. Methane Adsorption. Natural gas consists mostly of methane \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\right)\), which is a commonly used fuel gas with abundant reserves. Meanwhile, methane can also be produced from arguably renewable sources such as short-rotation biofuel crops. Although its combustion products contain \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), as do other hydrocarbons, methane has the lowest carbon composition among all of the hydrocarbons. Using methane as replacement for other current fossil fuels has attracted increasing attention. It is noteworthy that methane and its combustion product, \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), are also major greenhouse gases. Additionally, it is a major pollutant from current and future thawing of subarctic permafrost methane deposits due to climate change, also from anaerobic decomposition in landfills and from shale-gas hydraulic fracturing.\(^{247}\) Therefore, it is desirable to sequester both \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) from the atmosphere. Meanwhile, as for hydrogen, a major hindrance for the commercial application of natural gas as a vehicular fuel is the low efficiency for methane storage and delivery. The DOE has set the target for methane storage capacity at \(180\;\mathrm{v/v}\) (volume of absorbed methane at STP), in the temperature range \(-40\) to \(85\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and at a pressure below 35 bar. For light-duty vehicles with a reasonable driving range, the acceptable methane storage capacity of the absorbents (abs) must be at least \(0.5\;\;\mathrm{g_{CH4}/g_{abs}}\).\(^{248}\) Research exploiting PAFs as methane absorbents has also been conducted in view of their high surface area and stability.
The methane adsorption performance for some PAFs and related materials such as carbonized PAFs at ambient pressure and \(273\ \mathrm{K}\) were collated from the literature (Table 3). Apart from their hydrogen storage capacities, PAFs exhibit \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) heats of adsorption between 15 and \(20\;\mathrm{kJ\;mol^{-1}}\) at ambient pressure and \(273\ \mathrm{K}\) (Table 3). According to eq 1, \(\Delta S^{\circ}\) is approximately \(-9.5\mathrm{R}\) for methane storage, and at \(298\ \mathrm{K}\) the optimal enthalpy change is \(-18.82\ \mathrm{kJ\;mol^{-1}}\), which suggests that PAFs are apt candidates for methane storage at room temperature. Theoretical simulation of methane storage in PAF structures, using GCMC to reveal the interaction between PAF frameworks and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), has been applied to, for example, PAF-
\(30\mathfrak{n}\) with a dia net, with its \(C{-}C\) bonds replaced by up to four phenyl rings \(\left(n,\,n=1,\,2,\,3,\right.\) and 4). The optimal structures for methane storage indicated that an alkane−aromatic type interaction was the main one between methane and local aromatic motifs in those PAFs. Moreover, the simulation revealed that the calculated \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) binding energies (i.e., binding strengths) were higher for the biphenylmethane motifs (binding energies of 13.24 and \(15.20\,\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}}\) , III and IV, respectively, Figure 19) than for biphenyl and phenyl motifs (binding energies of 8.51 and \(9.68\;\mathrm{~kJ~}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) , I and II,
respectively, Figure 19) due to multiple interactions. Therefore, the PAF structures with tetraphenylmethane building units are most likely promising methane absorbents across a wide range of pressures. The calculations also suggested that PAF-302 possesses the best methane storage capacity at pressures from 10 to 280 bar, this being due to its high density of aromatic motifs and large free volume.249 Assisted by simulation, the infrared and Raman spectroscopy analyses of \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) -absorbed and \(\mathrm{CD}_{4}\) -adsorbed PAF-302 indicated multiple interactions between the methane and phenyl motifs in PAFs. Methane molecules simultaneously interact with one or two phenyl rings in the models of monodentate and bidentate adducts, respectively.178 Framework interpenetration also influences methane adsorption performance, with good agreement between simulation and experiment.250 Besides using computational simulation to understand methane storage, there has been high-throughput screening of a large number of hypothetical PAF frameworks with high methane storage capacity, different building units, connectivity, and structural topology with high methane storage capacity. Significantly, exceptional methane storage occurred when there were methane−methane attractions within the framework, with their absorption sites \(4.2\textup{\AA}\) apart.251
Linear regression of absorption data from Table 3 shows a positive correlation between the surface area and methane uptake capacity at \(273\;\mathrm{K}\) and 1 bar. There is a general trend of increasing methane uptake of some PAF materials with increasing surface area (ror 19 samples, \(\mathrm{R}^{2}~=~0.69\) , \(\textbf{P}<\) 0.00001, Figure 20). Data points in Figure 20 can be grouped
according to their perpendicular deviation from the line of best fit. Two such groups are those above the line (positive deviation) and close to the uptake axis and those distinctly below the line (negative deviation): they have higher and lower heats of adsorption, respectively. The heats of adsorption of these two groups are labeled in Figure 20. This data spread for methane absorption fits with the finding for other gases, namely that both surface area and binding energy contribute to methane adsorption. To date, the best reported PAF-based absorbent at ambient pressure is the KOH activated carbonized PAF, K-PAF-1-600. At \(273\,\mathrm{~K~}\) and 1 bar, KPAF-1-600 possesses a surface area up to \(2325\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and a \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) for
methane of \(18.9\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}\). The methane uptake capacity is 53.5 \(\mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which equals \(0.038\ \mathrm{g_{CH4}/g_{abs}}\).
Methane storage capacities at low pressure are far below the DOE target, but using PAFs with high surface area as \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) absorbents under high adsorbing pressure naturally improves their uptake. PPN-3, which has a BET surface area of \(2840\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), has a methane uptake of \(0.192\ \mathrm{g_{CH4}/g_{abs}}\) at \(295\ \mathrm{K}\), 70 bar. Even higher is the methane storage capacity of \(0.207\ \mathrm{g_{CH4}/g_{abs}}\) at \(298\ \mathrm{K}\), 35 bar for K-PAF-1-750, which is the KOH activated and carbonized PAF-1 heated to \(750\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). It has the highest surface area among all the carbonized PAF-1 series, of \(2926\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Recently, PAF materials with BET surface areas exceeding \(5000\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) have been synthesized by building-unit engineering. Because of their exceptional surface areas, PAF-100 (BET surface area: \(5501\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) and PAF-101 (BET surface area: \(5114\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\)) were studied under high-uptake pressure and yielded methane uptake capacities of \(0.375\) and \(0.306\ \mathrm{g_{CH4}/g_{abs}}\) at \(298\ \mathrm{K}\) and 70 bar, respectively. Remarkably, two porous organic polymers named KPOP-1 and KPOP-2 are found to have similar building units and synthetic strategy to PAF-100 and PAF-101. KPOP-1 and KPOP-2 possess methane uptake capacities of \(0.450\) and \(0.515\ \mathrm{g_{CH4}/g_{abs}}\), respectively, at \(298\ \mathrm{K}\) and 80 bar. The latter value meets the DOE gravimetric target for onboard methane absorbents.
Most of PAFs and conventional porous solids possess rigid framework with microporosity that renders their adsorption to methane following the Langmuir behavior. For those PAFs with highly accessible surface, the majority of methane uptake happens at low relative pressure. However, the minimum releasing pressure for a real methane delivery device should be above the ambient pressure and is usually in the range of 3−10 bar. For rigid PAFs, their working capacities, which refer to the amounts of methane released between the highest and the lowest working pressure of the system, are much lower than their total uptake. Recent researches on MOFs and porous polymers have demonstrated that the framework flexibility is effective to achieve high working capacity. Specifically, a covalent organic polymer (COP-150) synthesized from benzene and 1,2-dichloroethane exhibits high methane working capacity of \(0.625\ \mathrm{g\ g^{-1}}\) and \(294\ \mathrm{L\ L^{-1}}\), with the working pressure cycle between 5 and 100 bar. Furthermore, COP-150 is cost-effective and its production is readily to scale up. The successful synthesis of flexible porous material opens up a new avenue to methane delivery and inspires PAF design and synthesis in the future.
4.1.3. \(\mathbf{CO}_{2}\) Capture. Because of the unabated increase in global atmospheric \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) concentration, initiated by anthropogenic emissions but also including that from positive feedback (e.g., from additional forest fires and droughts and temperature-induced soil carbon emissions), it is crucial to recapture \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from anthropogenic emissions and also to sequester some of those from positive feedback. Carbon dioxide capture and sequestration (CCS) using porous materials such as MOFs and POPs have been extensively investigated, and reviews on the topic include technologies, scenarios, and experimental and computational details. Investigations to date demonstrate the correlation between porosity/function and the performance in CCS. Therefore, in this section, we specifically discuss the state-of-the-art in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake by PAFs and compare them in order to inspire their further design, modulation, and functionality.
One factor in CCS is that \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules have a larger quadrupole moment than other components in anthropogenic gas emissions, such as flue gas, natural gas from wells, and syngas (Table 4). Therefore selective capture of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from these gas mixtures can be based on quadrupole−dipole interactions and porosities of various PAFs.
The aromatic motifs in PAFs provide physisorption sites for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) storage like they do for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage. PAFs that have only aromatic motifs in their framework generally exhibit \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacities below \(100\ \mathrm{mg\ g^{-1}}\) at ambient pressure and room temperature due to a lack of strong binding sites. To improve that uptake capacity and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) binding strength, it is necessary to find PAFs with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-philic functional groups. Although research has shown that such functionalization would effectively decrease the surface area and eventually reduce the gas adsorption capacity, a higher heat of adsorption would yield improved selectivity from gas mixtures, as desired in the separation of gas mixtures. Moreover, there are many examples that functionality plays a dominant role and the gas capacity increases despite a decrease in surface area.
Computational simulation can predict the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture performance of several structures based on the skeleton of PAF-1 plus different polar functional groups, including: hydrogen (PAF-1), amine \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\right.\) _PAF-1), methoxyl (MO_PAF-1), and dihydrofuran (DHF_PAF-1, as shown in Figure 21). Grand-canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) simulations of each of these structures suggest that PAF-1 possesses the highest storage capacity at elevated pressure at \(3000\ \mathrm{kPa}\). Although the incorporation of functional groups decreases the free volume, DHF_PAF-1 has the highest \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) binding
strength of the four. Consequently, DHF_PAF-1 also has the highest \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake capacity up to \(440~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}\) at ambient pressure and \(298~\mathrm{K}\). The selectivity for binary gas mixtures of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\) follows the trend DHF_PAF-1 > MO_PAF-1 > \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) _PAF-1 > PAF-1, especially at ambient to moderate pressures. It can be inferred from the simulation that, for enhancement of selectivity, the electrostatic interaction between \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and the framework is more important than porosity. In another study, GCMC simulation of high pressure \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption suggests that PAF-302 (the simulated structure of PAF-1) has the highest \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity at 40 bar and \(298~\mathrm{K}\). After modifying the framework, a pyrimidine linked to the PAF-302 framework via \(-\mathrm{NH}-\) improves the uptake of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) at 1 bar, and the capture of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) by \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\mathrm{PAF}\) at high pressure was as efficient as for PAF-302. Simulation at different pressures also indicates that the carboxyl group in PAFs promotes \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake at low pressure and that the N sites dramatically increase \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake at high pressure.
Electrostatic interaction-aided \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake was also demonstrated empirically. PPN-6, which is believed to be the same structurally as PAF-1, was grafted with sulfonate groups by post synthetic modification \(\mathrm{(PPN-6-SO_{3}H)}\), and its lithium salt \(\mathrm{(PPN-6-SO_{3}L i)}\) was also synthesized. Compared with nonfunctionalized PPN-6, \(\mathrm{PPN-6-SO_{3}H}\), and \(\mathrm{PPN-6-SO_{3}L i}\) both exhibit nearly a 3-fold increase in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake (Table 5). Adsorption selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) in flue gas of composition 15:85 \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) can be estimated by ideal adsorption solution theory (LAST). At \(295\,\mathrm{~K~}\) and 1 bar, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity of \(\mathrm{PPN-6-SO_{3}H}\) and \(\mathrm{PPN-6-SO_{3}L i}\) are 150 and 414, respectively. Both the polar functional group and the reduced pore size contribute to enhanced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake and preferential adsorption. Similarly, for PAF-18-OH, directly synthesized with a phenolic hydroxyl functional group that gives a \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) of \(28\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}\), which is almost double the \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) for PAF-1. After ion exchange of a proton by lithium, the \(Q_{\mathrm{st}}\) for PAF-18-OLi increases slightly to \(29.5\ \mathrm{kJ\mol^{-1}}\), but due to a decrease in pore volume by Li incorporation, the IAST selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) under simulated flue gas conditions increases from 34 to 129.
Inspired by enhanced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture from lithium incorporation, alkali metals and alkaline-earth metals were introduced by ion exchange into PAF-26-COOH, a carboxyl-functionalized PAF. The isosteric heats of adsorption for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) both increased, indicating improvement in binding strength between the metal sites and the gases. The metalation of PAFs causes an increase in IAST selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\). Cobalt was incorporated into sulfonated PAF-1, which improved its selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) at 14.27 (molar ratio \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}=50/50\)) and 12.36 (molar ratio \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}=15/85\)), respectively.
Nitrogen atoms with lone pair electrons have specific dipole−quadrupole interactions with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Therefore, another promising strategy to improve \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake and selective capture is to incorporate nitrogen sites within PAFs. This can be achieved by postsynthetic modification, direct synthesis, or by a combination of the two. Diethylenetriamine(DETA) has been grafted onto PPN-6 in a two-step reaction to yield PPN-6-CH2DETA, which possesses superior \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake capacity and selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) compared with sulfonated PPN-6. Furthermore, IAST calculations show that its selectivity for \(400\;\mathrm{ppm}\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) at 1 atm is on the order of 3.6 \(\times\,10^{10}\), indicating that polyamine-modified PPN could well be suitable for direct capture of the greenhouse gas \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) from
air. On the flip side, the "de novo approach" has been developed to directly synthesize PAF-1 with amine functionality. The directly synthesized PAF-1- \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) from a functional building unit possesses \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake and isosteric heat of adsorption comparable to PPN-6-CH2DETA. Empirical evidence shows that functionality other than surface area and synthetic procedure strongly influences gas adsorption performance. Nitro-functionalized PAF-1s have been synthesized either directly or by post modification. Both products exhibit enhanced affinity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) compared with PAF-1. However, a difference in effectiveness between the two has not yet been measured, but it should be noted that in postsynthetic modification, the surface area and degree of functionalization can be finely tuned to give a suitable balance between \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity and affinity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) selectivity.
Besides N-containing sites, other heteroatoms such as P and F have also been incorporated into the frameworks. Specifically, fluorine with its high electronegativity creates a strong dipole−quadrupole interaction. Direct synthesis of fluorinated PAF-1 (F-PAF1) from fluorinated tetraphenylmethane as functional building units leads to the even distribution of C−F dipoles in the framework. Compared with nonfluorinated PAF-1, the fluorinated framework of F-PAF1 exhibits improved \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) affinity, with its \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) isosteric heat of adsorption elevated to \(25\;\mathrm{kJ\;mol^{-1}}\), from \(15.6\;\mathrm{kJ\;mol^{-1}}\) for PAF-1.
Multiple functional sites would give PAFs even greater advantages. Computational simulation of porous materials with multiple binding functionalities such as \(-\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), \(-\mathrm{CH}_{3}\), \(-\mathrm{SO}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}\), and \(-\mathrm{OH}\) shows that multiple sites in one phase would operate synergistically to improve \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture capacity and selectivity. PPN-6 decorated with sulfonate ammonium multiple sites has been reported: the simultaneously present ammonium and sulfonate group leads to both an increase in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake capacity and a decrease in chemical interactivity. Therefore, \(\mathrm{PPN-6-SO_{3}N H_{4}}\) is promising for flue gas separation. PPN-101 contains tetrahedral building units connected by benzimidazole linkages and has a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity of up to 199. Although this selectivity is less than for the postmodified PPN-6 series, expensive catalysts and air-sensitive reagents are not needed.
Both small pore volume and high binding strength contribute to high selectivity for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over other gases. Another successful method to increase the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity of simulated flue gas is to impregnate PAFs with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)-philic materials such as polyethylenimine (PEI). This process reduces the pore volume of PAFs and enhances the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) heat of adsorption. The surface area and pore volume of PAF-5 impregnated with 40 wt % PEI are dramatically reduced, but the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity of 15% \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over 85% \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) is up to 2160 at 313 K (calculated using Henry's law). A temperature swing adsorption (TSA) process shows that the working capacity of PEI (40 wt %) \(\subset\) PAF-5 at 413 K and 1 atm is 11.1 wt % (124.86 mg g\(^{-1}\)). This means that PAF-5 impregnated with polyethylenimine is promising for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture (Figure 22). Porosity of a covalent organic polymer, COP-1, synthesized from the same building units as PAF-6, was enhanced by a hard templating method. Then amine impregnation in COP-1 led to an improved \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake and a dramatic increase in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity compared to the unmodified COP-1.
Carbonization of raw PAFs increases their gas adsorption performance. PAF-1 samples are converted to porous carbons when heated above 400 °C. Surface area and pore size decrease
with increasing temperature. However, the narrower pore size strengthens the interactions between the porous framework and gases. Thus, carbonized PAF-1 treated at \(450\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) exhibits a higher gas uptake capacity and heat of adsorption than the raw PAF-1. Indeed, the \(\bar{\mathrm{CO}}_{2}\) uptake capacity of carbonized PAF-1- 450 reached \(198\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) double that of PAF-1 under the same conditions. Carbonized PAF-1 also exhibits enhanced uptake and heats of adsorption for each of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2},\) \(\mathrm{CH}_{4},\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) .222 Another technique to further reduce the pore size of carbonized PAFs is to preintroduce extra carbon by adsorption and in situ polymerization.269 The carbonized PAF with the highest \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake to date is activated by KOH, a strong base: namely K-PAF-1-600 with a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capacity of \(316.3\;\mathrm{mg}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at 1 bar and \(273\;\mathrm{K}.\) The gas storage performance for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4},\) and their selectivity from a binary gas mixture has also been investigated. The highest methane storage capacity is \(38.3~\mathrm{mg}\) \(\mathbf{g}^{-1}\) , being at \(273\;\mathrm{K}\) and 1 bar for K-PAF-1-600, and the highest \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) storage of \(3.06\,\mathrm{\,wt\,\,\%}\) is achieved by increasing the treating temperature to \(750\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.\) .223 Extra porosity can be created by removal of Si, such as from carbonized PPN-4, which increases its porosity and thereby significantly increases its \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake capacity compared to the raw PPN-4.270 Current experimental results for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) storage and adsorptive separation performances related to the aforementioned PAFs, and their derivatives are summarized in Table 5.
Compared with the MOFs for CCS application, PAFs would show better stability for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture from highly humid environment. Furthermore, their frameworks are readily modified to introduce \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) -philic sites and thus improve their CCS performances. Currently, some PAFs have exhibited comparable \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture capacity to other porous solids like MOFs,271 but the latter still possesses advantages with their
high selectivity,272 ability in trace amount \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture,273 and low energy cost for regeneration.274 To achieve better \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) absorbents based on PAFs, their porous structures such as pore size and pore volume should be precisely modulated, and their framework chemistry should be carefully controlled to increase the framework affinity to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) . Moreover, many PAFs with high surface area are synthesized using expensive catalysts, thus the cost for large scale synthesis of PAFs are too high for practical application. Developing new coupling reactions for cost-effective synthesis is another important research topic for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) absorbents.
4.1.4. Adsorptive Separation of Hydrocarbon Mixtures. The \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\) and \(C_{3}\) alkenes in high-purity concentrations are used as feedstocks in many petrochemical syntheses such as high-quality polyethene and polypropene. They are also the most copiously produced chemicals in the petrochemical industry, with many downstream products. Alkene purification from mixtures of small gaseous hydrocarbons, including alkanes, alkenes, and alkynes, is currently performed by high-energy input cryogenic distillation at high pressure because their very similar boiling points makes standard distillation inapplicable. Adsorptive separation of hydrocarbons using nanoporous materials would require less energy than the process based on phase transformation, and therefore it has attracted intensive interest. MOFs are particularly promising absorbents, being able to separate similar components in hydrocarbon mixtures based on subtle differences. Hydrocarbon separation has progressed greatly using such strategies, for example, based on the pore size and the pore shape of MOFs275 or on the unsaturated metal sites.276 PAFs have high stability in industrial applications, but their main drawback for efficient separation of hydrocarbon mixtures is their relatively irregular pore shape and size, which limits precise molecular sieving. Instead, hydrocarbon separation can be based on differences in binding energy achievable by introducing active binding sites into PAFs via their adaptable framework. PAFs could be designed and synthesized to have adsorptive hydrocarbon selectivity and then, for example, used as absorbents in fix-bed processes such as breakthrough or pressure/temperature/vacuum swing adsorption.
PAFs synthesized from porphyrin building units provide metal coordinating sites and a suitably confined space for gas adsorption. Small hydrocarbon molecules including methane, ethane, ethylene, and propene are adsorbed by the porphyrin-based PAF-40 and by metal-coordinated PAF-40 (PAF-40-Fe and PAF-40-Mn). Comparing the adsorption isotherms of corresponding gas molecules revealed that the propane capacities of the porphyrin-based PAFs are the highest of all the gases, especially at low pressure. The affinity of these PAFs for propane over methane is, in part, attributed to their microporosity, which renders stronger binding energy to larger molecules, if all sizes can be accommodated within the pores. Alternatively, for similarly sized ethane and ethylene, the incorporation of metal sites in PAF-40-Mn and PAF-40-Fe provides metal−olefin interactions which enhance ethylene adsorption. IAST calculations suggest a selectivity of propane over methane up to a factor of 246.11 The \(\mathrm{Ag(I)}\) -sulfonate-functionalized PAF-1, PAF- \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{Ag},\) strongly binds the carbon−carbon double bond of ethylene by \(\pi\) -complexation, but there is a much weaker interaction between the framework and saturated hydrocarbons. PAF- \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{Ag}\) exhibits a higher ethylene capacity than do PAF-1- \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) and PAF-1, at \(296~\mathrm{K}\) and 1 bar (Figure 23). For ethane storage in the absence of the
unsaturated double bond, the trend is in the order PAF-1 \(>\) PAF-1-SO\(_3\)H \(>\) PAF-SO\(_3\)Ag, which matches the trend in their surface areas. This indicates that the surface area dominates when the gas does not interact strongly with the framework. The isosteric heat of adsorption for ethylene to PAF-SO\(_3\)Ag can reach 106 kJ mol\(^{-1}\). The proposed ethylene/ethane selectivity from IAST calculations is 27.277
Apart from using coordinative metal sites to differentiate between saturated and unsaturated bonds in olefin/paraffin mixtures, the polyimide, PAF-110 has been used to separate acetylene and ethylene. Computational simulation gave binding energies of \(-22.8\,\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}}\) for acetylene hydrogen compared with \(-16.1\;\mathrm{kJ\;mol^{-1}}\) for ethylene hydrogen. Because of the stronger binding energy of carbonyl oxygen to hydrogen in acetylene than in ethylene, PAF-110 exhibits IAST selectivity for acetylene/ethylene at 4.5, surpassing some of the reports for MOFs.131
4.1.5. Capture of Ammonia. Ammonia \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)\) is naturally ubiquitous, but exposure to even slightly elevated concentrations such as tens of parts per million (ppm) can cause health problems in humans. Therefore, removal of ammonia at ppm or even ppb-level concentration from the environment is of particular interest, but it remains a challenge. Additionally, ammonia is a corrosive gas and some MOFs that are good adsorbents for other gases are unstable in the presence of ammonia.278,279 In contrast, PAFs possess high surface area and good physicochemical stability to strong acids and bases, making them potential ammonia adsorbents.
Physical adsorption processes that have a relatively low enthalpy of adsorption \(\left(\Delta H_{\mathrm{ads}}\right)\) are too unsuitable for the capture of trace amounts of ammonia, especially in high humidity or in the presence of other competing gases. Functionalized PAFs with ammonia binding sites would increase \(\Delta H_{\mathrm{ads}}\) by forming chemical bonds, and then they could selectively capture ammonia at low concentrations. Ammonia is a Lewis acidic with a strong affinity for Lewis acids. Metals with Lewis acidity have been anchored inside catechol-functionalized porous organic polymers, which then act as ammonia adsorbents. At \(80\%\) relative humidity (RH), Zn- and Cu-incorporated porous organic polymers exhibit 3.32 and \(4.32\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) of ammonia uptake, respectively, which is much higher than for zinc oxide- and amine-impregnated porous carbon.280 A porous organic polymer based on polyimide (NU-POP-1) was subsequently tested for uptake of ammonia and other toxic industrial chemicals under both low and high humidity.171 The microbreakthrough experiment shows that the NU-POP-1 possesses a saturated ammonia loading of 5.56 mol \(\mathrm{kg}^{1-}\) at \(0\%\) RH and \(6.17\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\) at \(80\%\) RH, and it thereby indicates the combined contributions of water and oxygen for ammonia adsorption in the framework.281
Long et al. proposed a strategy for binding ammonia within a PAF framework by way of multiple chemical interactions. In that study, they synthesized a series of frameworks based on functionalized PAF-1 containing Brønsted acids such as sulfonate and carboxyl groups. From the consequent ammonia adsorption at room temperature for the PAFs with isolated Brønsted acidic sites, one can infer that it is the acidity rather than the surface area that dominates adsorption performance. The higher ammonia uptake for the MOFs with the same Brønsted acidic sites in the corresponding functional PAFs provides cogent evidence for this, because the carboxylate groups in the ligands of the MOFs increase the acidity of the isolated Brønsted acidic sites via an electron-withdrawing effect. On the basis of the idea of increasing enthalpy of adsorption by multiple chemical interactions, cooperative carboxyl functionalized PAFs (BPP-5 and BPP-7) were designed and synthesized. The cooperative functional group is achieved and fine-tuned by framework interpenetration and by the steric effect of protective substitution (Figure 24). As a
result, BPP-5 exhibits an ammonia uptake as high as 17.7 mmol \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at ambient pressure.282 This work highlights the importance of the functional group's steric arrangement on gas adsorption in porous frameworks.
## 4.2. Membrane Separation
Membrane-based separation is a continuous process with a lower energy consumption than adsorptive separation, which proceeds via discrete adsorption/desorption cycles and uses energy to regenerate the absorbent. Polymeric membranes are used industrially, but a common and undesirable trade-off for their gas separation performance is that two important parameters for membranes, the permeability and selectivity, are limited by the Robeson upper bound.283 Porous solids with molecular-scale windows are able to separate different molecules depending on their shape and size, which is
known as the "molecular sieving" effect. Extensive research has shown that the membranes based on nanoporous materials, such as zeolites and MOFs, are able to exceed the nominal upper bound.\[284-287\] Porous materials with ordered structures such as zeolites, MOFs and COFs are synthesized via reversible reactions. Their growth processes are thermodynamically controlled, which means that they can be produced in bulky solid forms, such as membranes and monoliths. In contrast, POFs are synthesized by irreversible reactions and are usually produced in the form of powdered solids under kinetic control. A hurdle for practical application of PAFs is that they cannot be cast into different shapes (the "processable" problem). Therefore, POFs have been combined with polymeric membranes to produce mixed-matrix membranes (MMMs).\[288-290\] Using organic frameworks, especially PAFs, as the additives in MMMs has several advantages, including humidity resistance, due to the good physicochemical stability of PAFs; and the surface compatibility between PAFs and polymers. Polysulfone (PSF) hollow fibers with PAF-56P as an additive have been fabricated. The preferential adsorption for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) in the nitrogen-containing PAF-56P renders a competitive separation for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) at \(293{-}353\;\mathrm{K}\) for the PAF-56P/PSF membrane, with separation selectivity up to 38.9 and a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability of 151 GPU (1 GPU = 1 × 10\(^{-6}\) cm\(^3\) cm\(^{-2}\) s\(^{-1}\) cmHg\(^{-1}\) at standard temperature and pressure, STP).\[155\] Experimental investigations of MMMs with PAF-1 additives are genuinely promising, both for gas separation and exciting improvements to membrane stability. Pioneering work was reported by Hill et al. when they investigated the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) separation by a super glassy polymer with PAF-1 as an additive.\[291\] The addition of PAF-1 within matrices such as poly(trimethylsilylpropyne) (PTMSP), poly(4-methyl-2-pentyne) (PMP), and PIM-1 leads to enhanced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability due to the extra diffusion channels within the highly porous PAF-1. Fortuitously, the physical aging phenomenon (viz. porosity loss of the matrix over time) is abated by the addition of PAF-1. After 240 days, PTMSP loses \(38\%\) of its original \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability, but in comparison, the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability of the PTMSP/PAF-1 membrane is only reduced by \(7\%\) under the same conditions. The fact that this antiaging action occurs in PAFs but not MOFs might be due to the surface compatibility between PAFs and the polymers. Solid-state NMR shows some unchanged side chains of PTMSP after aging, which suggests a strong interaction between PAF-1 and the saturated aliphatic chains. Therefore, the conclusion is that PAF-1 is tethering the pendant side chains of the polymer and fixes the polymeric backbones in place (Figure 25). The antiaging effect of PAFs is general to various PAF structures: the time-dependent gas separation performance of MMMs with PAF-11 additives in membranes with different thicknesses was studied in detail, and there is indeed a similar antiaging effect.\[292,293\]
