In the situation where PIM-1 plays the role of a matrix, the incorporation of PAF-1 leads to selective antiaging: the permeability of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) is virtually unaltered after 400 days but the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) permeability is reduced by \(90\%\) of its original value. Consequently, the selectivity of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) over \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) is accordingly increased and an improved gas separation is achieved with a PAF-1/PIM-1 mixed-matrix membrane (Figure 26).\[294\]
Combined computational simulation (GCMC and molecular dynamics) suggests that the large pore size of diamondoid PAF-1 may reduce its gas selectivity in membrane-based separations.\[96\] Subsequently, higher membrane selectivity for use with gas mixtures was achieved by deploying functionalized
PAFs with tailored properties. The use of amine or sulfonate functionalized PAF-1, or PAF-1 infiltrated with \(C_{60}\) or \(\mathrm{Li}_{6}\mathrm{C}_{60}\), as additives in a PTMSP membrane dramatically enhances its gas permeability. This is due to the enhanced affinity of functionalized PAFs for gases such as \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\). Meanwhile, PTMSP ages less in the presence of the rigid, functionalized, and highly porous PAFs.\[295\]
PAF-based membranes are studied for separation of both gas- and liquid-phase mixtures, and the latter is implemented by processes such as nanofiltration, osmosis, and pervaporation. For instance, PAF-56 has been incorporated in a polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) membrane, resulting in MMM with an enhanced affinity for \(n\)-butanol in a water/\(n\)-butanol system. The PAF-56/PDMS membrane exhibits improved flux and selectivity for \(n\)-butanol compared with the raw PDMS.\[296\] An antiaging effect similar to that for the gas separation membranes was also observed in pervaporation membranes. PTMSP membranes incorporated with PAF-1 or hyper-cross-linked polymer (\(p\)-DCX) additives exhibit improved selectivity, ethanol flux, and antiaging when applied to an ethanol/water
mixture.297 The same antiaging membranes are also used for organic solvent nanofiltration, yielding high ethanol flux and dye rejection.298
For desalination, a microporous carbon material from the carbonization of a \( C_{60} \) impregnated PAF-1 \(\left(\mathrm{C}_{60} @ \mathrm{PAF}_{900}\right)\) was incorporated into a polyamide (PA) film for forward osmosis (FO). The incorporation of \( \mathrm{C}_{60} @ \mathrm{PAF}_{900} \) led to improved water transport and thus an increased water flux.299 The amendable character of PAFs has also led to an antifouling property for membranes. PAF-C, which has the same structure as PAF-6, reacts with 1,3-propane sultone to yield zwitterionic PAF (Z-PAF-C). The loading of Z-PAF-C into polyamide (PA) membranes improves the membrane hydrophilicity, which in turn increases water flux during desalination. Meanwhile, because of the electrostatic repulsion between the electronegative membrane surface and the negative charged foulants, the Z-PAF-C/PA membrane exhibits obvious antifouling effects for model foulants such as bovine serum albumin and humic acid.300
The permeability of MMMs increases steadily with increasing amounts of porous additives. This is because the porous additives provide sufficient channels for permeating molecules. However, the selectivity of MMMs may exhibit a maximum because at high concentrations the additives are incompatible (phase separation) with the polymer matrices. Continuous PAF membranes constructed entirely from the intergrowth of porous organic frameworks can be expected to possess improved separating performance by both selectivity and permeability. MOFs and zeolite are grown from reversible solvothermal/hydrothermal procedure, thus the intergrowth of crystals ensure the formation of defect-free MOF/zeolite-based membranes. Despite the poor processability of PAFs, there are several reports of the direct synthesis of POF membranes using superacid-catalyzed synthesis of CTF membranes301 or the surface-initiated polymerization of CMP membranes.302 These provided inspiration for the direct synthesis of PAF membranes, for which new synthetic strategies will present themselves. Further research interests also include investigations on the mechanical behavior of the PAF membrane. The MOF and zeolite membranes made from intergrowth crystals usually exhibit poor mechanical stability. In contrast, PAF materials are believed to be flexible and tough, therefore its membrane form should have better mechanical stability for practical application, but specific investigations should be carried out.
## 4.3. Adsorption of Hazardous Organic Compounds
4.3.1. Capture of Hazardous Organic Chemicals. As indicated by their building units and structures, PAFs possess a high density of aromatic motifs in their framework. Because of \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction, the expectation is that PAFs would show higher affinity for aromatic compounds than for saturated hydrocarbons. This was endorsed by early research in the field, not least of all by showing that some PAFs had excellent adsorption capacities for benzene and toluene.31,79,103 Also, large differences in uptake capacity between benzene and cyclohexane have been observed, pointing to the possibility of selective capture of aromatic compounds from organic solvents.104 JUC-Z1, which has octaphenylsilsesquioxane (OPS) structural motifs in its framework, exhibits significant uptake of benzene at a relative pressure below 0.1, indicating the benzene molecules move into the micropores. Compared to uptake of benzene, the water vapor adsorption isotherm for JUC-Z1 at the same relative pressure shows almost no uptake of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and low uptake even at the saturated vapor pressure. Considering the stability of PAFs in aqueous solutions and in organic solvents, the differences in the isotherms between benzene and water suggest that hydrophobic, nonpolar channels can be created within PAFs and used for the capture of organic compounds and in water purification.83 A series of PAFs synthesized from the combination of tetrahedral building units and triangular building units were reported, and their suitability for organic dye removal was tested. Two examples are noteworthy: (a) the highest adsorption capacity for rhodamine B was \(1666~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}\), achieved by the small-pore PAF-111,38 and (b) a low-cost PAF-45 with a high benzene ring density was used to absorb phenol303 and perfluorooctanesulfonate (PFOS) from water.304
JUC-Z1 at the same relative pressure shows almost no uptake of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and low uptake even at the saturated vapor pressure. Considering the stability of PAFs in aqueous solutions and in organic solvents, the differences in the isotherms between benzene and water suggest that hydrophobic, nonpolar channels can be created within PAFs and used for the capture of organic compounds and in water purification.83 A series of PAFs synthesized from the combination of tetrahedral building units and triangular building units were reported, and their suitability for organic dye removal was tested. Two examples are noteworthy: (a) the highest adsorption capacity for rhodamine B was \(1666~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}\) , achieved by the small-pore PAF-111,38 and (b) a low-cost PAF-45 with a high benzene ring density was used to absorb phenol303 and perfluorooctanesulfonate (PFOS) from water.304
Besides using nonfunctionalized PAFs to capture hazardous organic compounds based on \(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions, the amenable frameworks of PAFs also offer diverse functionality for task-specific capture of target molecules. Sulfonate has been grafted into the framework of JUC-Z2 to create acidic sites. Consequently, amine compounds can be selected by the acidic JUC-Z2- \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) in preference to less basic nitrogen-containing compounds. The acid−basic interaction between JUC-Z2- \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) and amines is affected by solvent polarity, and amines were released on application of high polarity solvents.305 Similar polarity effects and solvent competition occurs in PAF80 and PAF-80-SO3H, which have polar pores: their channels are modified by hydroxyl in PAF-80 and by sulfonate in PAF-80-SO3H, respectively, to give PAFs with different wettability and polarity. These polar PAFs efficiently capture antibiotics from water. Furthermore, the polarity matching between the framework and adsorbates is important for absorbing targeting antibiotics.189 Besides the hydrophilic PAFs with polar channels, hydrophobic PAFs that have high uptake to organic solvent also show their potential in organic compound capture. A Covalent organic polymer (COP-177) synthesized from perfluoroarenes possesses hydrophobicity and microporosity. It is able to separate the mixture of organic compounds with similar boiling points. Two petrochemicals, 1-methylnaphthalene and heptamethylnonane, can be separated depending on their size in a continuous column of COP-177.50 Another fluorinated network (COP-99) was synthesized from the selfcondensation of perfluorohydroquinone and was able to purify the charged dye molecules from water.306 The fluorine is the key functional group of the network and provides the fluorination interactions to selectively adsorb charged molecules with suitable size. The charge-specific and size-dependent properties of COP-99 provide the possibility for the applications in desalination and separation.
Reducing sulfur emission from fossil fuel combustion is achieved by chemical transformation and adsorptive desulfurization. Chemical transformation of benzothiophene and its derivatives requires extreme operating conditions and consumption of chemicals such as oxidants and catalysts. Therefore, adsorptive desulfurization is a promising technology for green chemistry and a more sustainable environment. PAFs with ionic frameworks can selectively capture organosulfur compounds due to their cationic groups with a polarity affinity. The ionic PAF, iPAF-1, with a moderate BET surface area of \(594~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) exhibits a dibenzothiophene (DBT) uptake up to \(769~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}}\). Molecular dynamic simulation suggested that the high adsorption capacity is due to a suitable binding energy between the imidazolium motif and DBT.143
However, most of the PAFs are powders, and this creates a problem for operational usage and recycling. Therefore, PAF-based monolith materials must be prepared for particle-type applications. In an attempt to incorporate PAF-1 into cellulose nanofabric aerogels, and to firmly fix the PAF-1 within the aerogel, a dual cross-linked method was developed. Up to 50 wt \(\%\) PAF-1 powder can be incorporated in the aerogel while keeping its integrity. The PAFs within aerogels had several advantages over the powder-form of PAFs. Because of the extremely low density of the aerogel, the composite possesses a high BET surface area of \(2200\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which is beneficial for a high uptake of bisphenol A (BPA), and the high connectivity in the gel facilitates rapid capture. At least \(77\%\) of the BPA in water is adsorbed within \(10\ \mathrm{s}\), and it is completely removed within an hour. The composite is readily recyclable, and the removal efficiency remains constant after several cycles of use and recycling. An electrospun polyacrylonitrile (PAN) fiber membrane was coated with polyaniline (PANI) as a seed layer, and PAF-45 was directly synthesized from the seed layer to enhance the porosity of the fiber membrane. PAF-45 was strongly anchored at the surface of the electrospun fiber through covalent bonding. The PAF-modified fiber membrane was used to remove water contaminates with practicable operability.
4.3.2. Enrichment of Trace Organic Compounds for Analysis. Solid-phase microextraction (SPME) is an important sample-enriching and pretreatment technique used in gas chromatography (GC). PAFs that efficiently enrich the organic compounds being examined are used as absorbents in SPME. Several examples are listed here to indicate the range of methods and applications. PAF-1/ionic liquid (IL) hybrid composites have been coated onto the surface of quartz fibers to create PAF-based SPME fibers. The fibrous PAF-1/IL is capable of enriching organochlorine pesticides from the environment, and it can detect the corresponding chemicals in trace amounts. PAF-48 was coated on silica fibers via the sol−gel process. Because of abundant phenyl rings in the PAF-48 framework, SPME fibers are effective at extracting aromatic volatile compounds including styrene, benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, and xylenes. This is facilitated by being similar polarity and by the "\(\pi\)-\(\pi\) affinity" interactions. PAF-6 is synthesized from building units of piperazine and cyanuric chloride and can be processed into nanopourous carbons (PAF-6-NPC) via pyrolysis. SPME fibers formed by physically coating stainless steel wires with PAF-6-NPC can effectively preconcentrate \(n\)-alkanes from water and soil for use in GC measurement. Some other diverse organic separation examples are PAF-6, which can be used to extract and determine trace concentrations of bisphenol A from milk via the "capture and elute" method, and PAF-56 microspheres, which were physically coated on stainless steel wires to extract triazole fungicides.
## 4.4. Capture of Inorganic Chemicals
4.4.1. Capture of Metals for Environmental Remediation and Detection. MOFs are one of the most effective absorbents of gas molecules and organic compounds due to their advantageous high surface area and tunable surfaces. However, when trialed as absorbents for metal capture from aqueous solution, their hydrolytic stability hampers their performance. Another issue that MOFs face is the possibility of metal exchange between the framework and the adsorbate. Although research into metal exchange in MOFs is expanding
in such fields as heterogeneous catalysis and structure modulation, their metal composition clashes with capturing and measuring other metals. In contrast, PAFs without metal components in their structures provide a suitable platform for metal capture and detection.
Heavy metals are of concern in human health care and more generally in the environment. Their effective removal by adsorption is readily operational and inexpensive. The main challenge is to improve the performance of the adsorbents in such areas as selectivity, efficacy, and recyclability. PAFs with task-specific functions could address these challenges by functional-led design and synthesis. Some examples of successful adaptation of PAFs follow. The thiol group, which strongly chelates mercury, can be anchored in PAF-1 to give PAF-1-SH, which then has \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\)-capturing sites distributed inside accessible nanopores. PAF-1-SH can trap \(\mathrm{Hg}(\mathrm{II})\) with a fast adsorption rate, high uptake, high selectivity, good recyclability, and high removal efficiency. PAF-1 modified by an ammonium functional group gives a rigid cationic framework that was used as an ion exchange material. The framework prevents possible swelling and entrainment of conventional ion-exchange resins, and it has a high capture or removal efficiency for metal complex ions and metal oxoanions. Besides the simple functional groups with metal capturing properties, a metal-coordinated fragment with pyridine and carboxyl was decorated in a PAF skeleton, inspired by molecularly imprinting technology. Because of the existence of molecular imprinting sites, PAF, with regularly assembled pyridine and carboxyl groups, exhibits high selectivity for Pb(II) over other competing metal ions.
Metals that occur naturally in animal tissues are normally maintained within specific concentration ranges, and values outside of those ranges can indicate diseases. PAFs can be used for medical diagnoses applications by selective capture and monitoring of chemical concentrations in biological fluids. In aiming at a potential diagnostic tool for Wilson's disease, Chang et al. reported the capture of copper by a thioether-functionalized PAF material, PAF-1-SMe, from biofluids such as urine and serum (Figure 27). PAF-1-SMe possesses copper
capture ability, with a distribution coefficient 10 times higher than for any of the earlier best copper adsorbents. In biofluids more complex than urine, such as serum, PAF-1-SMe shows good selectivity for copper over other metals such as zinc and iron. The amount of adsorbed copper can be determined by colorimetric analysis of a solution of 8-hydroxyquinoline and
PAF-1-SMe(Cu). This combined "capture and measure strategy" has proven to be a promising diagnostic tool.317 The same methodology through a simple operation was carried out to capture and measure the iron in groundwater. The ether−thioether functionalized PAF, PAF-1-ET, is able to capture aqueous Fe ions with high selectivity and efficiency. The capture site for Fe is oxygen coordinating in a pseudooctahedral mode, which was confirmed experimentally by Mössbauer analysis and X-ray absorption spectroscopy and by computational simulation. The method of measuring copper content by 8-hydroxyquinoline-assisted colorimetric analysis can also be used for Fe analysis at high accuracy, without any more complicated operations or expensive equipment.318
When handling radioactive waste from nuclear reactors, it is important to effectively separate the lanthanide and actinide ions from the first row of transition metals and their fission products. The interpenetrated PAF material with multiple carboxyl groups, BPP-7, has been used for separation of these metal ions, including \(\mathrm{Sr}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\), \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\), and \(\mathrm{Am}^{3+}\), from aqueous solutions. From the adsorption isotherms of the single metal ions \(\mathrm{Sr}^{2+}\), \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\), and \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\), one can infer that \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\) is the most strongly bound by the framework, through multiple interactions with carboxyl groups. Ideal adsorbed solution theory based on Langmuir isotherm fits shows that selectivity for \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\) over \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) or \(\mathrm{Sr}^{2+}\), with \(5\%\) \(\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}\) at \(0.1\,\mathrm{mM}\) total concentration, reaches 350 and 130, respectively.319
4.4.2. Extraction of Uranium from Seawater. Uranium is of particular importance to the nuclear power industry, but calculations based on its consumption rate and known geological reserves suggest that terrestrial uranium reserves can only last for another century. In contrast there is nearly 1000 times more uranium in the ocean but at levels of parts per billion (ppb). Extracting uranium from seawater has been under development for decades, but significant hurdles remain, including discriminating uranium from the other metals present and degradation of the adsorbents by seawater.320 Any solutions to these issues would be considered world-changing chemical-separation technologies.321
Because of the amendable nature and high stability of PAFs, they have been tested for uranium extraction efficacy by several research groups using either simulated or real seawater. Poly(acrylonitrile) (PAN) has been grafted onto the surface of a diamondoid porous aromatic framework (PPN-6) via atom-transfer radical polymerization. The PAN was further amended to include the effective uranium capture functional group, poly(amidoxime) (PAO), via treatment with hydroxylamine. The uranium uptake capacities of this PPN-6-PAO from simulated seawater with 6 ppm uranium content are 64.1 and \(63.2\mathrm{~g~U~kg^{-1}~a d s}\), respectively, at room temperature or \(80\,^{\circ}\!C\). For filtrated seawater with a spiked uranyl concentration of \(80\,\mathrm{\ppb}_{\mathrm{}}\), the uptake capacity is \(4.81\,\mathrm{~g~}\) U \(\mathrm{kg^{-1}}_{\mathrm{ads}}\) after 42 days.203 Ma et al. synthesized an amidoxime-functionalized PAF-1 and reported a maximum uranium uptake of \(304\,\mathrm{~mg~U~g^{-1}}_{\mathrm{ads}}\). Amidoxime-PAF-1 is kinetically efficient at reducing the uranium concentration in aqueous solution from \(4.1\,\mathrm{ppm}\) to \(1.0\,\mathrm{ppb}\). X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) spectroscopy suggested a chelating effect by the adjacent amidoxime groups to the uranyl ions \((\mathrm{UO}_{2}{}^{2+})\).202 Although the functional group of amidoxime (AO) is widely recognized as promising uranium chelating sites in polymers, recent investigation has found that uranium capture within the nanosized space of PAF-1 can be further improved by
modifying the flexibility and grafting degree of the AO-containing groups. AO-modified PAF-1 with a higher active-site density and higher functional chain length results in cooperative coordination to uranium by neighboring AO groups, thus improving the uranium uptake capacity and efficiency.322
In real seawater, there are many interfering ions that cause poorer uranium selectivity. To improve the capture-selectivity of PAFs, a molecularly imprinting model based on a uranyl-coordinating complex has been doped into a PAF framework to give a molecularly imprinted PAF. The existence of targeting metal-chelating groups significantly enhances the selectivity for uranium over other interfering ions. Also, the porous framework provides a large accessible surface area beneficial to uranium capture (Figure 28). The molecularly imprinted
PAF exhibits a 4-fold increase in uranium capacity compared with molecularly imprinted nonporous polymers, and it has the highest uranium selectivity of all the reported adsorbents.195
4.4.3. Adsorption of Nonmetallic Compounds. PAFs have been widely investigated for metal adsorption and their capture sites are suitably designed and well established. The target metals are usually captured within the frameworks by coordination bonds or electrostatic interactions. In comparison, adsorption of nonmetallic compounds has been studied much less because the capture sites have to be designed individually for different compounds. Because of the growing importance of environmental and energy issues, it would be ideal to synthesize PAFs that can effectively capture some crucial nonmetallic compounds.
Removal of boric acid from water by adsorption is a positive alternative to conventional reverse osmosis, which faces an ongoing battle to improve boron rejection in water purification. Inspired by commercial boron adsorption resins
with polyol groups, two PAF materials have been functionalized using \(N\)-methyl-D-glucamine (NMDG) as capture sites. Both the NMDG modified PAFs showed boron capture far superior to that of the commercial boron adsorption resins regardless of the presence of competitive ions. The functional PAFs rapidly reduced the boron contents in simulated seawater from \(2.91\;\mathrm{ppm}\) to below the detection limit of \(0.05\;\mathrm{ppm}\). The high loading content of NMDG in highly porous PAFs gives a high boron uptake capacity and rapid adsorption rate. A range of complexation models of NMDG with boron species exists and contributes to the adsorption, as indicated by the \({}^{11}\mathrm{B}\) MAS NMR.\(^{323}\)
Iodine isotopes are a major radioactive waste product from nuclear power generator. They are highly volatile and react readily with other airborne organic chemicals. Therefore, it is necessary to develop efficient adsorbents for iodine capture and storage.\(^{324}\) PAFs with high surface area and physicochemical stability have been deployed as iodine absorbents. For example, PAF-1 and N-containing JUC-Z2 were synthesized and studied for iodine capture. Because of the ultrahigh surface of PAF-1, it has the highest iodine uptake capacity of all the porous absorbents, at \(1.36\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Iodine uptake by JUC-Z2 at \(1.44\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) was slightly lower than for PAF-1, but the electron-rich framework of the former provides strong interaction with the electrophilic iodine. The isosteric enthalpy for \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\) in JUC-Z2 is \(-51.1\;\mathrm{kJ}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\), which is much higher than that for PAF-1 at \(-14.9\;\mathrm{kJ}\;\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\). Thus, JUC-Z2 has a higher adsorbing selectivity for iodine over water than does PAF-1.\(^{325}\) Iodine uptake was further enhanced to \(2.6{-}2.7\;\mathrm{g}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) by introducing a negative charge into the framework. Comparison of multiple PAFs showed that the charge center (the boron ion), phenyl rings, and acetylenic bonds in PAFs all contribute to iodine adsorption.\(^{109}\)
## 4.5. PAFs for Catalysis
Heterogeneous catalysis possesses significant advantages over homogeneous catalysis in terms of their catalyst recovery and recycling, and they are therefore highly favored in industrial processing. However, the efficiencies of heterogeneous catalysts versus the corresponding homogeneous ones are often limited due to having fewer accessible active sites. Porous solids possess a large accessible surface area, and therefore they should make ideal heterogeneous catalysts or catalyst supports. Zeolites have been used as commercial catalysts in petroleum refinement due to their robust frameworks and existing Brønsted acidic sites. The organic ligands in MOFs are available for functionalization, thus providing versatile catalytic possibilities. Although the catalytic application of MOFs is to a certain extent limited by relatively weak coordination bonding, they have been keenly viewed for transformation to higher value products. PAFs with their covalent linkages and aromatic motifs as building units combine the advantages of both zeolites and MOFs to give simultaneous high stability and chemical variety. Furthermore, PAFs possess highly lipophilic pores, which are potentially suitable for organic transformations. Catalytic applications of PAFs have been extensively studied. PAFs constructed only of phenyl rings are catalytically inert. To give PAFs catalytic activity, task-specific functionalization is necessary. Many functional groups play the role of catalytic sites, and these can be introduced into PAF frameworks by postsynthetic modification. For example, PAFs functionalized by sulfonate and amino groups are used as solid acid and base catalysts, respectively. As demonstrated in
sections 3.1 and 3.2, acidic and basic sites can be introduced into PAF frameworks by simple treatments. Besides these simple acidic or basic sites, other functionalities can be introduced into PAFs because of their frameworks' modifiability and stability. For instance, phosphazene groups can be functionalized in a porous polymeric aromatic framework with an adamantane core (PPAF-AD) by three steps of postmodification. The resulting PPAF-AD-Phos exhibits an increased turnover number for ring opening polymerization and is readily recyclable compared to the homogeneous phosphazene catalyst.\(^{326}\) These functionalities ensure their wide applications in various catalytic reactions such as cascade catalysis, asymmetric catalysis, redox catalysis, and photocatalysis. In this subsection, these applications of PAFs have been summarized. Discussion about hierarchically structured PAFs is also included because it is an important topic in heterogeneous catalysis.
4.5.1. PAFs for Cascade Catalysis. In many instances, the antagonist functional groups such as acids and bases catalyze reactions continuously in a multistep (cascade) reaction. To avoid neutralization of the antagonist sites when in solution, the sites can be spatially separated to a degree. Two monofunctionalized PAFs, one embedded with acidic sites and the other with basic sites, can be mixed to provide catalysts for cascade reactions. The acid-functionalized and base-functionalized PAF are mixed together, but neutralization does not occur due to their spatial separation. The mixture shows a high conversion rate and high selectivity in the cascade-type deprotection-aldol reaction.\(^{327}\) Also, the porous polymeric aromatic framework (PPAF) was modified with sulfonate groups and amino groups to give a bifunctional material, PPAF-SO\(_3\)H-NH\(_2\) (Figure 29), for a cascade reaction.
PPAF-SO\(_3\)H-NH\(_2\) with the multiple functional sites within one material exhibits high conversion and high selectivity compared with the physical combination of several materials each with a single functionality.\(^{125}\) A similar idea is used in the synthesis of dual functionalized PAF-1 for a one-pot reaction. Bifunctional PAF with amine groups and sulfonate groups is used as a catalyst for a range of cascade reactions such as the deacetalization Henry reaction and the deacetalization Knoevenagel reaction.\(^{328}\)
4.5.2. PAFs for Asymmetric Catalysis. Asymmetric catalysis is an important research topic in catalysis. Chiral catalytic sites have been introduced into porous frameworks to give heterogeneous catalysts that promote organic reactions with enantioselectivity. A chiral prolinamide catalytic site was
in fixed in the PAF-1 framework via multistep-postsynthetic modification. The resulting material exhibited good diastereoselectivity and enantioselectivity for the Aldol reaction between \( p \)-nitrobenzaldehyde and cyclohexanone. The investigations described above confirm complex catalytic functionalities after postmodification.
4.5.3. Porphyrin PAFs for Redox Catalysis. Catalytically active building units pass on their abilities for organic transformation to the PAF framework, which then provides significantly improved catalytic activity. A typical example that has been widely studied is porphyrin, which is one of the rigid, square building units used in PAF synthesis. Related to this moiety are metalloporphyrin complexes, which are the functional analogues of heme cofactor and are recognized as enzyme mimics for catalyzing oxidation reactions. In view of this, syntheses of PAFs constructed from metalloporphyrin building units are a topic of vigorous research as is their application as heterogeneous catalysts. The first report of biomimetic catalysis based on the porphyrin framework was the covalent heme framework (CHF-1), which is synthesized by the direct Yamamoto coupling of an iron(III) porphyrin heme complex. The density of porphyrin in CHF-1 is high, and the catalytic efficiency in oxidation reactions is comparable to the natural enzyme, horseradish peroxidase.
PAF-76 is synthesized from cross coupling of porphyrin building units and tetraphenylmethane based tetrahedral building units. Additionally, metals such as Fe, Mn, and Zn are coordinated by the porphyrins to create PAF-76-M. Separation of the porphyrins by the tetrahedral nodes ensures that the metal sites are isolated from each other. PAF-76-M consequently exhibits improved catalytic performance in the styrene oxidation reaction, including a high conversion turnover frequency and high selectivity for the product phenylacetaldehyde.
4.5.4. PAFs for Photocatalysis. Photocatalysis induced by visible light is a sustainable catalytic process. For oxidative reactions, a photosensitizer that converts oxygen to a more reactive oxygen species such as singlet oxygen or superoxide radicals is required. Porphyrin and metalloprophyrin which are also effective photosensitizers were employed as building units for photocatalytic PAFs. A tin-porphyrin-based PAF was synthesized via the Yamamoto-type Ullmann coupling reaction and used for aerobic oxidation of sulfides. Through a comprehensive characterization process, it was proven that the tin-porphyrin in the PAF's skeleton activated molecular oxygen to form singlet oxygen and superoxide radicals. The tin-porphyrin-based PAF converted various sulfides to the corresponding sulfoxides with a high turnover frequency and high selectivity. The catalysts also exhibited good recyclability without losing their catalytic efficiency after at least four runs.
4.5.5. Catalysis by Metal Loading. Metal catalysis and organometallic catalysis are vigorous research topics. Within these fields, avoiding the aggregation of nanoparticles and thereby increasing the efficiency of heterogeneous catalysis by anchoring metal and organometallic catalysts on the surface of porous materials is itself a vigorous research topic. PAFs with high surface area or effective metal binding sites therefore have wide application in heterogeneous catalysis.
The rigid framework of PAFs keeps their included metal particles within a constrained volume and in spatial isolation. Ruthenium nanoparticles were formed in situ within diamondoid PAFs, distributed in narrow pores. High resolution
transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images showed the existence of the ruthenium nanoparticles inside the PAFs and with uniform sizes correlated to pore size. These nanoparticles are used to catalyze size-selective hydrogenation of various aromatic substances. In the situation where the interaction between the metal and the framework is weak, then the metals may leak out and the nanoparticles may aggregate. Therefore, it is necessary to introduce metal binding sites into the PAF frameworks to improve their catalytic performance. These sites include coordinating atoms or motifs, as well as ligands from organometallic chemistry. Representatives of the former include the nitrogen- or phosphorus-containing PAFs. Ultrafine Pt nanoparticles have been impregnated and dispersed in a porous organic polymer, which is termed PCPOP and synthesized from piperazine and cyanuric chloride (same as PAF-6). The nitrogen atoms in PC-POP strongly anchor the metal to produce Pt@PC-POP. Catalytic performance in the reactions of selective hydrogenation of halogenated nitrobenzenes and of ammonia borane hydrolysis showed excellent catalytic activity and good recyclability of the catalysts. Moreover, no metal leakage or aggregation was observed. Recently, phosphine-based PAFs have been synthesized (PAF-93 and PAF-94) from the building units triphenylphosphine \(\left(\mathrm{PPh}_{3}\right)\) and dipheny-2-pyridylphosphine \(\left(\mathrm{PPh}_{2}\mathrm{Py}\right)\). Because of the existence of the strongly binding P and N sites, gold nanoparticles are immobilized in PAF-93 and PAF-94 and they showed high catalytic kinetics for nitrophenol reduction. Similar to other metal catalysts anchored within PAFs, the recyclability of the composite catalyst is improved compared with gold alone, as evidenced by the retention of catalytic activity after several reaction cycles.
A different type of metal loading site was inspired by the chemistry of metallorganic catalysis. Two PAFs, one with imino-pyridine and the other with chiral proline, as coordinating groups (PAF-NPy and PAF-NPro, respectively) were synthesized from the amine functionalized PAF (PAF-NH\(_2\)). Both PAFs were treated with copper(I) and rhodium(I) to give metal-complex embedded catalysts. Compared with homogeneous organometallic catalysts, the PAF-based catalysts exhibit similar catalytic efficiencies for different organic reactions. Meanwhile, they also exhibit the known advantages of heterogeneous catalysts, such as high durability, good recyclability, and easy manipulation in solution. Subsequently, proline functionalized PAFs, partially incorporated with rhodium, namely amine/metal-complex bifunctional PAFs, were used for the tandem Knoedt-nagel-hydrogenation reaction.
N-Heterocyclic carbene (NHC) of transition metal complexes is an important ligand in organometallic catalysis. Immobilization of NHC in PAFs has been achieved by postsynthetic modification through multiple steps. The NHC moiety, with iridium and ruthenium complexes, has been prepared and used to catalyze organic reactions such as amine alkylation and transfer hydrogenation. The results indicate that infixing of metal-NHC in PAFs retains the complex's catalytic activity and that the catalysts can be readily recovered by filtration without any obvious loss of their catalytic ability.
PAFs can acquire complicated structures and functions by introducing multiple functionalities. Molecularly imprinted PAFs have been designed and synthesized to mimic the activity of naturally occurring organophosphorus hydrolase, with its high structural complexity. The aim was to mimic the hydrolysis of the nerve agent paraoxon. The hydrolysis sites
were created by infixing the PAF with molecular imprinting motifs that contain the coordinating zinc complex of the paraoxon transition-state analogue. Simultaneously, the hydrolysis product adsorption sites were similarly infixed. The result is a PAF with both hydrolysis sites and adsorption sites (Figure 30). The resultant molecularly imprinted PAF has a higher catalytic rate than all the artificial enzymes and the natural organophosphorus hydrolase.148
4.5.6. Hierarchical Catalysts. Microporous frameworks provide large, accessible surface areas where catalysis can occur. However, mesoporosity is preferred in heterogeneous catalysis as the mesoporous frameworks promote diffusion of substances during reactions. Moreover, many organocatalysts molecules are too bulky to enter the micropores and can only be accommodated in mesoscale voids. Therefore, synthesis of hierarchical structures, which consist of both micropores and mesopores, is a topical field of research. The pore size of PAFs can be tuned by selecting the building units and the linkages, with larger pores created from larger molecules, allowing creation of PAFs with hierarchical frameworks. In section 2, we demonstrated the direct synthesis of mesoporous PAF-70-NH\(_2\) from boronic-acid-containing tetrahedral building units and amine-functionalized 4,4′-dibromobiphenyl. The porosity assessment of PAF70-NH\(_2\), based on nitrogen adsorption isotherms. suggests that it possesses mesopores of width 3.8 nm. Encouraged by the existence of these mesopores, PAF70-NH\(_2\) was further modified with covalently linked bulky thiourea units (PAF70-thiourea, Figure 31). The mesoporous channel that was available for substance diffusion in PAF70-thiourea improved its catalytic performance for NBS-mediated oxidation of alcohols compared to its homogeneous counterpart. The high recyclability of PAF-70 is further evidence for the strong binding of its catalytic sites. PAF70-thiourea could sustain at least 36 cycles without impairment of catalytic activity.137 The mesoporous cages of PAF-70-NH\(_2\) are also suitable for the immobilization of Pd nanoparticles. The nanoparticles are uniform in size and high loading contents are possible within PAF-70. The Pd-impregnated PAFs showed high catalytic activity in Suzuki−Miyaura cross coupling reactions.136
Rigid and bulky building units have proven their positive contribution to the synthesis of mesoporous frameworks. Apart from the designed synthesis, construction of hierarchical structures with mesopores and micropores has also received inspirational input from framework kinetics. Cationic imidazolium groups grafted into tetrahedral building units have been applied to directly synthesize iPAF-1, which has an ionic
framework. Because of the steric hindrance of the imidazolium motifs, iPAF-1 exhibits micropores smaller than the pores in PAF-1 but with a similar diamondoid framework. Anionic polyoxometalate (POMs) clusters with oxidative activity can be incorporated into frameworks as guest molecules. The nitrogen adsorption isotherm of the POMs incorporated in iPAF-1 exhibits an increased surface area and an obvious hysteresis loop, which indicates that the structure has changed from only microporous to a micromesoporous hierarchical framework. Conversely, a rigid cationic framework after POM loading showed a loss of surface area rather than an increase as did iPAF-1.335 It is believed that the POM clusters with a multivalence charge number are strongly attached to more than one hanging imidazolium cations from the adjacent cages via an electrostatic interaction and some of the cages are left empty, which results in mesoporous voids, as illustrated by small-angle X-ray scattering (SAXS) and molecular dynamics (MD) simulation (Figure 32). Catalytic tests of oxidative desulphurization for iPAF-1 showed improved conversion rates benefiting from improved substance diffusion. The hierarchical framework induced by guest loading in iPAF-1 has proven that the framework kinetics of PAFs can be utilized for structural tuning.
4.5.7. Challenges for PAF-Based Catalysts. Functional PAFs and PAF-based composites possess versatile framework chemistry. To a certain extent, they can achieve most of the functions of other porous heterogeneous catalysts such as zeolites and MOFs. Moreover, they have better stability than a majority of MOFs. Therefore, they are considered as promising catalysts for vast heterogeneous reactions. A current drawback of PAF-based catalysts is that they do not have unique pore size distribution as those crystalline porous solids such as zeolites and MOFs. The unique pore size of crystalline porous catalysts leads to the size or shape dependent effects to substrates and significantly enhances the selectivity of reactions. It is necessary to synthesize PAFs with well-defined framework to develop better heterogeneous catalysts as well as understanding their structure−property correlation in the future.
## 4.6. PAFs as Nanoreactors
The rigid framework of PAFs provides confined spaces at the nanoscale, making them promising hosts for single molecules or clusters. Certain reactions within confined spaces may exhibit modified reactivity and production selectivity. As a proof-of-concept, PAF-1, with its high surface area and rigidity, has been used as a nanoreactor for dehydrogenation and polymerization. Ammonia borane (AB), which is a chemical-absorbent candidate for hydrogen, is accommodated in PAF-1 with a weight ratio of approximately 1:1. Compared with unconfined AB, the dehydrogenation temperature of the confined AB is dramatically reduced to \(77\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and its dehydrogenation rate is accelerated. Moreover, the confinement of AB in PAF-1 also leads to improved selectivity for hydrogen production. No detectable volatile compounds, such as ammonia, diborane, or borazine, were observed as byproducts by temperature-programmed desorption mass spectrometry. All evidence points to modulation of the reactivity of the confined molecules by PAF-1 acting as a nanoreactor.336 Polymerization of acrylonitrile also occurs in situ within the nanospaces of PAF-1. The product is believed to be a composite material with two distinct phases (Figure 33). The successful implementation of this conceptual strategy will allow polymer materials to be operated at nanoscales and it should engender innovative properties.337
## 4.7. PAFs for Sensing
Quantification and selective detection of target chemicals can be performed if a material changes one or more of its properties in response to the addition of those chemicals, according to a measurable correlation. The properties of the material that are affected by the "analytes" might be, for example, the electrochemical, optical, or mechanical properties, and so on. Sensing based on porous materials with their large number of accessible sites available to react with analytes is especially attractive because of the high sensitivity. MOFs have been very successful as chemical sensors due to their high surface area and structural variety. One affected property is fluorescence, detectable by simple testing operation. The so-called "turn-off" or "turn-on" sensing is realized in some PAF
materials that have fluorescent properties due to fluorescent motifs or building units in their framework. For instance, PAFs synthesized from highly fluorescent building units were used to detect nitro-containing aromatic compounds, which are typically used as explosives. The fluorescence of PAFs was quenched by the electronegative nitro groups of the explosives.338,339 Intrinsic fluorescent features of PAFs, such as the emission wavelength, photoluminescence quantum yield, and orbital energy level, can be tuned by modifying the composition of the fluorescent building units to provide selective sensing of specific explosives.340 Besides directly introducing fluorescent building units into the PAF framework, fluorescent PAFs can also be obtained by post modification. A fluorescent molecule, 5-carboxyfluorescein (SCF), has been infused into an amine-functionalized PAF-1, giving the fluorescent PAF-5CF, which exhibits an obvious fluorescent emission, either in the solid state or when dispersed in water. The fluorescence of PAF-5CF is quenched by the addition of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\), with high sensitivity and high selectivity. The effective quenching of PAF-5CF in response to \(\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}\) arises from competition for light absorption between the analyte and the PAF. This example shows that a sensing functional group can be introduced into porous, stable frameworks to create solid-state sensors.198
Framework dynamics can be observed by deuterium spin−echo NMR measurements. The intense rotation of the phenyl motif makes the PAF-3 framework a molecular rotor, and its rotational frequency can be modulated by incorporating guest molecules such as \(\mathrm{I}_{2}\).180 Such a special dynamic phenomenon within the frameworks has inspired research into turn-on sensing. Four porous organic frameworks (NUS-20, -21, -22, and -23) were synthesized with tetraphenylethylene (TPE) molecules as the building units. The molecular dynamics of TPE was restricted by the incorporation of volatile organic
compounds (VOCs), and this led to fluorescence originating from aggregation-induced emission (AIE). The turn-on sensors based on framework dynamics exhibited good linear correlations between the fluorescent intensity and the VOC concentrations over a wide range of analyte concentrations.341
## 4.8. Medical Applications of PAFs
PAF-6, which was synthesized from the nucleophilic substitution reaction of cyanuric chloride and piperazine, shows a layered hexagonal structure with plenty of N sites and a high surface area. Subsequently, the in vitro drug uptake and release performance of PAF-6 has been investigated, and ibuprofen was used as the model drug. PAF-6 released \(50\%\) of adsorbed ibuprofen within \(5\ \mathrm{~h~}\) and complete release was achieved after \(46\,\mathrm{~h~}\).80 Although there is concern about the biocompatibility of PAFs with aromatic motifs, the extremely strong connection between those aromatic motifs ensures there would be no leakage of harmful small molecules into biofluids. Investigations into the toxicity of the external surface of PAFs toward the tissues and possible effects on metabolism are required for the in vivo bioapplication of PAFs.
The cationic PAF-50, which contains quaternary pyridinium sites and chloride anions as counterions, was deployed in an antibacterial application. Silver(I) was evenly distributed within the pores of PAF-50. It was released slowly due to the presence of chloride ion. Furthermore, the organic framework of PAF-50 is highly compatible with conventional polymers and can be readily applied as a coating by the casting method. The antibacterial coating based on functional PAFs is efficient and durable and has great potential for use in hospitals and other medical settings.342
## 4.9. PAFs and Their Derivatives for Electrochemistry
In recent years, rechargeable batteries have attracted much attention as a power supply, in part as they show promise to replace of fossil fuels. The Li−S battery, with a high energy density, low cost, and environmental compatibility, is particularly attractive, but the formation of soluble lithium polysulfides causes a severe decline in capacity. Despite their low electrical conductivity, investigations have demonstrated that porous frameworks that can effectively confine S are able to overcome the undesired shuttle effect in Li−S batteries and extend the life cycle.343 The high porosity and stability of PAFs encourages research focusing on their use in Li−S batteries. An electron-rich PAF with triphenyl amine building units has been used as a host material to accommodate and confine the S, with a weight ratio of \(32\%\). Discharge−charge testing showed that the electron-rich PAF retained \(83\%\) of its initial reversible capacity after 50 cycles, indicating a promising solution to the shuttle effect of Li−S batteries.344 Recently, the S loading content was increased to \(72\ \mathrm{wt}\%\) in a different N-containing PAF (PAF-41), which has a hierarchical porous structure. The cell had a high capacity at \(725.8\ \mathrm{mAh\ g^{-1}}\) in the first cycle and sustains a reversible capacity of \(491.4\ \mathrm{mAh\ g^{-1}}\) after 500 charge−discharge cycles, which equals a low decay rate of \(0.06\%\) per cycle.345
The use of a PAF-based electrode for electrochemical conversion was also investigated. The tetrahedral carbazole-containing building units were polymerized by oxidative coupling to give the electroactive PAF-39, which was formed into a modified glassy carbon electrode. It exhibited a strong catalytic current peak toward \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) reduction and a linear response to changes in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) concentration within the range \(5{-}50\ \mathrm{mM}\).157
Incorporation of metal nanoparticles is found to be helpful in increasing the conductivity of electrode materials and in improving electro-activity, as many carbon-based PAFs are unaffected by electric fields. Palladium nanoparticles have been supported on the surfaces of PAF-6 to fabricate an electrochemical electrode material. The nitrogen-rich framework and high surface area of PAF-6 provides a substantial number of immobilizing sites for Pd and contributes to a uniform distribution of particle size. Pd nanoparticles were uniformly infixed in the PAF-6 framework as spheres of \(2\ \mathrm{nm}\) diameter. Differential pulse voltammetry measurement of a Pd/PAF-6 electrode response to vanillin showed a linear relationship over concentrations from 10 to \(820\ \mathrm{pM}\). The electrochemical behavior of the Pd/PAF-6 electrode toward vanillin indicates that biomolecules in real-world applications can be detected with good selectivity by PAFs with immobilized metal nanoparticles and that these have good stability and reusability.346 When replacing the Pd nanoparticles by Au nanoparticles, the Au/PAF-6 electrode was found to effectively detect quercetin in a similar fashion to vanillin detection with Pd.347
For electrochemical applications, the conductivity of the electrode material is of paramount importance but it is usually low for PAFs, which hinders their use in electrochemical reactions. A simple and straightforward solution is to carbonize the PAF materials to create carbonaceous materials. The carbonized PAFs acquire the high surface area of the original PAFs. As mentioned in section 4.1.1, the carbonized PAF-1 with KOH activation shows a BET surface area of \(2926\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and a micropore volume of \(1.14\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), both of which are much higher than for the directly carbonized PAF-1. K-PAF-1 was used as an electrical double layer capacitor and the capacitance reached \(280\ \mathrm{F\ g^{-1}}\) in KOH aqueous solution, which exceeds the capacitances of commercially activated carbon, mesoporous carbon, and carbon nanotubes under similar conditions.348 PAF-6, with its extra carbon source, was treated at high temperature and the derived carbon material retained the original heteroatoms from the PAF-6 framework. The N-rich carbon has proven to be an active electrocatalyst for the oxygen-reduction reaction (ORR), with about \(40\ \mathrm{mV}\) higher onset potential than the commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) catalyst.349 The carbonized PAFs are also promising materials for lithium-ion350 and lithium sulfur batteries351 due to their high porosity, which accommodates the charge carriers. Because of the high surface area and the good gas adsorption capacity, the carbonized PAF-1 was used as a molecular tank to induce the oxygen incorporation in electrochemical active \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) Nanosheets. The carbonized PAF-1 (CPAF) with captured oxygen participated in the synthesis of \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) and led to the enlargement of the interlayer distance between the adjacent nanosheets. The \(\mathrm{O-MoS}_{2}\)-CPAF exhibited improved conductivity and modified electron transportability due to the oxygen incorporation and the presence of carbonized PAFs. The electrocatalyst was used for hydrogen evolution reaction with improved catalysis performances and good stability.352
## 4.10. Stimuli-Responsive PAFs
Diverse functional motifs can be incorporated within PAFs directly or through post modification. Simultaneously, the voids allow certain alterations to the configuration of the motifs with the property of stimuli-responsiveness. Therefore, the functionalized PAFs containing those motifs may respond to multiple stimuli such as light, heating, electric field, and
chemical environments. Spiropyran is a molecule that responds to ultraviolet (UV) and visible light with structures that reversibly open and close their rings. Building units with spiropyran motifs have been introduced into PAF frameworks by direct synthesis. The resultant PAFs showed switchable metal capture and release in response to light irradiation and chromism control by multiple stimuli including acid and solvents (Figure 34).353
Functional groups with azo motifs exhibited a reversible trans−cis isomerization that is triggered by UV irradiation or heating. It is expected that the change of the configuration of azobenzene inside porous frameworks would affect the gas adsorption performance and the framework porosity. Therefore, building units containing azobenzene motifs have been exploited to create stimuli responsive PAFs. The porosity assessments based on the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption isotherms indicate an increase of the surface area and pore size distribution of the azobenzene-containing PAF-36 and PAF-37 after UV irradiation. Furthermore, both PAFs exhibited reversible alternation on \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake triggered by UV light and regeneration by heating, which is caused by changes of their geometries and dipoles in azobenzene.354
Alternatively, stimuli-responsive motifs can be located within PAFs as guest molecules thanks to their high porosity. A photoirradiation-responsive molecule, diarylethene (DArE), has been incorporated in PAF-1 with a resultant high surface area. The conformation of parallel open-ring DArE is preferred in the confined nanopores of PAF-1 due to aromatic stacking and hydrogen bonding. Photoirradiation promotes the formation of the closed-ring form of DArE, and it weakens the interaction between \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and its adsorption sites. The conformation transformation induced by photo irradiation provides the first reported irradiation-triggered \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture and release, which is attractive for industrial applications.355 Stimuli-responsive PAFs open the gateway to development of next-generation smart materials.
## 5. CONCLUDING REMARKS AND PERSPECTIVE
The past decade has witnessed rapid development of research into PAFs. In the early stages, that research began with the design and synthesis of new PAF structures. Subsequently, investigations into their applications proliferated, from the conventional applications in adsorption, separation, and catalysis to more advanced ones such as nanoreactors, sensing, and stimuli-responsive smart materials. PAFs are hugely attractive due to their framework stability and function modifiability. The functionalization of PAFs importantly combines the synthesis and the applicability. Function-led design provides PAFs that have the desired properties. In many cases, targeted-directed synthesis, functionalization, and applications of PAFs behave in an integrated manner, snowballing development in this field.
In most cases, the functionalization of high-surface-area PAFs causes an obvious decrease in their porosity. The postmodification of PAF-1 with even a simple functional group dramatically decreases the surface area. For example, sulfonate-functionalized PAF-1 has a BET surface area of \(1254\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which is a reduction of \(70\%\) of the BET surface area of the original PAF-1.40 The nitro-functionalization of PAF-1 to PAF-1- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) decreases the surface area by \(58\%\), from \(4162\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for PAF-1 to \(1764\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) for PAF-1- \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\).328 Although it is reasonable to consider that the importation of extra species increases the density of the original framework, a new functionalizing strategy that can retain the high porosity is important for producing functional, porous materials with ideal performance. Further understanding of the correlation between the functional sites and surface is necessary. Development of new reactions for PAF synthesis is another intriguing topic. Current coupling reactions for PAF syntheses use organometallic catalysts which are expensive and difficult to remove from the framework. The Pd-based organometallic catalysts which are extensively used for Suzuki or Sonogashira coupling reactions are constrained within the resultant PAF framework in the form of nanoparticles. It is hard to remove these particles as they are insoluble in common acidic or basic solutions. Their presence blocks the channels of the framework, increases the apparent density of the PAFs and decreases the porosity. Additionally, the cost of \(\mathrm{Ni}(0)\) and Pd catalysts increase the price of PAF synthesis and hinders their use at an industrial scale. Synthesis of PAFs with high porosity and low cost is crucial to their practicable application.
As PAFs are synthesized from irreversible coupling reactions and their structures are cross-linked in all dimensions, they cannot be melted or dissolved in most commonly used solvents. Unlike conventional methods used to shape polymers and MOFs via casting and growth dynamic control, respectively, PAFs are obtained as solid powders and considered as the materials with inherent poor processability. A commonly faced challenge is to engineer their morphologies and shapes for specific applications, such as tubular nanostructures, microspheres, and core−shells for catalysis, or bulky solid forms such as membranes and films for separation. Current research into surface-initiated polymerization for CMP membranes302 and PAF coatings308 offers inspiration for shaping PAFs for future industrial applications. Together with other porous frameworks, PAFs with specific functions and properties will secure their contributions in the field of advanced materials.
